PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L04U6.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":4,"cefr":"A2b","unit":6,"theme":"Celebrations and Traditions","essential_question":"How do families and communities celebrate together?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["Eid","Ramadan","Founding Day","guest","gift","invite","decorate","lantern","feast","tradition","celebrate","gather","relatives","hospitality"],"recycled_words":["family","grandfather","neighbor","dates","coffee","happy","share","both","different","theme","adjective","lantern","souq"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS1","title":"Word Power: Celebrations and Traditions","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about celebrations and traditions and 3 Tier 2 words (culture, unique, symbol)","understand multiple-meaning words (date, present, light, ring, wave) and choose the meaning that fits the sentence","talk about how families celebrate Eid and Founding Day"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن الاحتفالات والتقاليد و3 كلمات أكاديمية.","فهم الكلمات متعددة المعاني واختيار المعنى المناسب للجملة.","التحدث عن احتفال الأسر بالعيد ويوم التأسيس."],"success_criteria":["I can read and use the celebration words in sentences.","I can explain two meanings of a multiple-meaning word.","I can use context to choose the right meaning."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع قراءة كلمات الاحتفال واستخدامها في جمل.","أستطيع شرح معنيين لكلمة متعددة المعاني.","أستطيع استخدام السياق لاختيار المعنى الصحيح."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"Eid","pos":"noun","ipa":"/id/","definition":"a Muslim celebration; Eid al-Fitr comes after Ramadan and Eid al-Adha comes during Hajj","arabic":"عيد","example":"We wear new clothes on Eid.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"Ramadan","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈræməˌdɑn/","definition":"the Islamic month when Muslims fast from dawn to sunset","arabic":"رمضان","example":"We break our fast together in Ramadan.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"Founding Day","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfaʊndɪŋ deɪ/","definition":"a Saudi national day on February 22 that remembers the start of the first Saudi state in 1727","arabic":"يوم التأسيس","example":"Our school celebrated Founding Day with a show.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"guest","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɡɛst/","definition":"a person you invite to your home","arabic":"ضيف","example":"We served coffee to our guests.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"gift","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɡɪft/","definition":"something you give to someone to make them happy","arabic":"هدية","example":"Grandma gave me a gift for Eid.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"invite","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪnˈvaɪt/","definition":"to ask someone to come to your home or an event","arabic":"يدعو","example":"We invited our neighbors to lunch.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"decorate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈdɛkəˌreɪt/","definition":"to make a place look beautiful for a special day","arabic":"يزيّن","example":"We decorated the house with lights.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"lantern","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlæntərn/","definition":"a light in a box or a frame with glass or paper sides","arabic":"فانوس","example":"A lantern glowed by the door.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"feast","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fist/","definition":"a large, special meal for many people","arabic":"وليمة","example":"The family had a feast after the Eid prayer.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"tradition","pos":"noun","ipa":"/trəˈdɪʃən/","definition":"something people have done in the same way for many years","arabic":"تقليد / عادة","example":"Giving eidiya to children is an Eid tradition.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"celebrate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈsɛləˌbreɪt/","definition":"to do something special and happy for an important day","arabic":"يحتفل","example":"We celebrate Eid with our relatives.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"gather","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈɡæðər/","definition":"to come together in one place","arabic":"يجتمع","example":"The family gathered at Grandpa's house.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"relatives","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛlətɪvz/","definition":"people in your family, like aunts, uncles and cousins","arabic":"الأقارب","example":"We visit our relatives on Eid.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"hospitality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌhɑspəˈtæləti/","definition":"being friendly and generous to guests","arabic":"كرم الضيافة","example":"Saudi families are famous for their hospitality.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"culture","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkʌltʃər/","definition":"the ideas, art, food and ways of life of a group of people","arabic":"ثقافة","example":"Arabic coffee is part of our culture.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"unique","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/juˈnik/","definition":"the only one of its kind; very special","arabic":"فريد","example":"Every country has unique Eid traditions.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"symbol","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪmbəl/","definition":"a picture or thing that stands for an idea","arabic":"رمز","example":"The palm tree is a symbol of Saudi Arabia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **multiple-meaning word** is one word with **two or more meanings**. It is spelled and often said the same way, but the meaning changes in different sentences.\n\nCelebration examples: **date** (a sweet fruit / a day on the calendar), **present** (a gift / here now), **light** (a lamp / not heavy), **ring** (jewelry for your finger / the sound of a bell), **wave** (move your hand to say hello / a moving line of water in the sea).\n\nSteps: (1) Read the **whole sentence**. (2) Think of **all the meanings** you know. (3) Try each meaning in the sentence. (4) Choose the one that **makes sense** with the other words (the context). A dictionary entry lists meanings with numbers.","examples":["*Grandma put a bowl of **dates** on the table.* → the sweet fruit.","*What is the **date** of Founding Day? It is February 22.* → a day on the calendar.","*My uncle gave me a **present** for Eid.* → a gift.","*Everyone was **present** for the Eid photo.* → here, not absent.","*Noura turned on the **light** inside the lantern.* → a lamp.","*The paper lantern was **light**, so Dana could carry it.* → not heavy.","*The guests **ring** the doorbell.* → make a bell sound. / *Aunt Huda wore a gold **ring**.* → jewelry.","*The children **waved** to the guests.* → moved their hands. / *A big **wave** splashed us at the beach.* → moving water.","Tip: look at the **part of speech**: *a ring* (noun) vs. *ring the bell* (verb)."],"common_errors":["✗ Choosing the first meaning you know. → ✓ Try every meaning in the sentence. (Context decides.)","✗ Everyone was gift for the photo. → ✓ Everyone was present for the photo. (present = here.)","✗ We celebrate Eid in our relatives. → ✓ We celebrate Eid with our relatives. (celebrate **with** people.)","✗ The guests was happy. → ✓ The guests were happy. (Plural → **were**.)","✗ We decorated the house by lights. → ✓ We decorated the house with lights. (decorate **with**.)","✗ I invite my cousins yesterday. → ✓ I invited my cousins yesterday. (Past tense **-ed**.)","✗ Eid Mubarak to all of you guests. → ✓ Eid Mubarak to all our guests! (Keep it simple.)"],"arabic":"الكلمة متعددة المعاني كلمة واحدة لها معنيان أو أكثر، تُكتب وتُنطق غالبًا بالطريقة نفسها لكن معناها يتغير بحسب الجملة.\n\nأمثلة: date (تمرة / تاريخ)، present (هدية / حاضر)، light (ضوء / خفيف)، ring (خاتم / يرنّ)، wave (يلوّح / موجة).\n\nالخطوات: اقرأ الجملة كلها، وفكّر في كل المعاني، وجرّب كل معنى، واختر المعنى المناسب للسياق.\n\nانتبه لنوع الكلمة: a ring اسم (خاتم)، وring the bell فعل (يقرع الجرس)."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Eid Mubarak! Today, we learn the words of celebrations and traditions.","on_screen":"Celebrations and Traditions 🏮","visual":"Noura hangs a lantern; Saqr wears a tiny party hat."},{"time":"00:20–01:10","narration":"Ramadan, Eid and Founding Day! We invite guests, decorate the house, gather with relatives and share a feast!","on_screen":"Eid · Ramadan · Founding Day · guest · invite · decorate · gather · relatives · feast","visual":"A family decorates, welcomes guests and shares a big meal."},{"time":"01:10–01:40","narration":"Traditions, gifts, lanterns and hospitality – they are part of our culture.","on_screen":"tradition · gift · lantern · hospitality · culture · unique · symbol","visual":"Arabic coffee is poured for guests; a child receives a gift."},{"time":"01:40–02:30","narration":"Some words have two meanings! A date is a fruit… and a day on the calendar!","on_screen":"date 🌴 / date 📅","visual":"A date fruit flips into a calendar page."},{"time":"02:30–03:00","narration":"Read the sentence, try each meaning and choose the one that fits!","on_screen":"present 🎁 / present ✋","visual":"A gift box flips into a hand raised in class."}],"captions_ar":["عيد مبارك! اليوم نتعلم كلمات الاحتفالات والتقاليد.","رمضان والعيد ويوم التأسيس! ندعو الضيوف ونزيّن البيت ونجتمع مع الأقارب ونتشارك الوليمة!","التقاليد والهدايا والفوانيس وكرم الضيافة جزء من ثقافتنا.","بعض الكلمات لها معنيان! date تمرة… وتاريخ في التقويم!","اقرأ الجملة، وجرّب كل معنى، واختر المعنى المناسب!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, joyful female voice, speed 0.90. Noura introduces the celebration words."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","instructions_ar":"اختر الكلمة الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"The Saudi day on February 22 that remembers the first Saudi state is ___.","answer":"Founding Day","explanation":"Founding Day.","explanation_ar":"يوم التأسيس.","options":["Ramadan","Founding Day","a feast","a lantern"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Aunts, uncles and cousins are your ___.","answer":"relatives","explanation":"Relatives.","explanation_ar":"أقاربك.","options":["guests","relatives","symbols","gifts"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"We hung paper stars and lights to ___ the house for Eid.","answer":"decorate","explanation":"Decorate.","explanation_ar":"نزيّن.","options":["gather","invite","celebrate","decorate"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A large, special meal for many people is a ___.","answer":"feast","explanation":"Feast.","explanation_ar":"وليمة.","options":["gift","lantern","feast","date"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Pouring coffee and serving dates for visitors shows ___.","answer":"hospitality","explanation":"Hospitality.","explanation_ar":"كرم الضيافة.","options":["hospitality","temperature","a budget","a symbol"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"We sent cards to ___ our neighbors to the Eid lunch.","answer":"invite","explanation":"Invite.","explanation_ar":"ندعو.","options":["invite","decorate","gather","wave"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Multiple-meaning words. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الكلمات متعددة المعاني: اختر.","items":[{"q":"*Grandma put a bowl of **dates** on the table.* What does *dates* mean here?","answer":"sweet fruits","explanation":"Context: bowl, table.","explanation_ar":"تمر.","options":["days on the calendar","sweet fruits","meetings with friends","numbers"],"dok":1,"skill":"multiple meanings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*What is the **date** of Founding Day?* What does *date* mean here?","answer":"a day on the calendar","explanation":"Context: of Founding Day.","explanation_ar":"تاريخ.","options":["a day on the calendar","a sweet fruit","a palm tree","a party"],"dok":1,"skill":"multiple meanings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*Everyone in the class was **present** for the Eid party.* What does *present* mean here?","answer":"here, not absent","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"حاضر.","options":["a gift","a show","here, not absent","late"],"dok":2,"skill":"multiple meanings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*The paper lantern was so **light** that baby Dana could hold it.* What does *light* mean here?","answer":"not heavy","explanation":"Context: Dana could hold it.","explanation_ar":"خفيف.","options":["a lamp","bright","a color","not heavy"],"dok":2,"skill":"multiple meanings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In which sentence does *ring* mean *jewelry for your finger*?","answer":"Aunt Huda wore a gold ring.","explanation":"Noun meaning.","explanation_ar":"خاتم.","options":["Ring the bell at the door.","The phone will ring soon.","Aunt Huda wore a gold ring.","Did you hear the bell ring?"],"dok":2,"skill":"multiple meanings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*The children **waved** to the guests from the car.* Which sentence uses *wave* with the SAME meaning?","answer":"Sami waved goodbye to his grandfather.","explanation":"Same meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى نفسه.","options":["A big wave splashed the boat.","The waves at the beach were tall.","We swam over a wave.","Sami waved goodbye to his grandfather."],"dok":3,"skill":"multiple meanings","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Arabic coffee and dates are part of Saudi ________.","answer":"culture","explanation":"Way of life.","explanation_ar":"الثقافة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Every family has its own ________ way to celebrate.","answer":"unique","explanation":"Special, one of a kind.","explanation_ar":"فريدة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The lantern is a ________ of Ramadan nights in many countries.","answer":"symbol","explanation":"Stands for an idea.","explanation_ar":"رمز.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Giving eidiya to children is an old Eid ________.","answer":"tradition","explanation":"Done for many years.","explanation_ar":"تقليد.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"On Eid morning, all our relatives ________ at Grandpa's house.","answer":"gather","explanation":"Come together.","explanation_ar":"يجتمعون.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"We ________ Founding Day with a school show every year.","answer":"celebrate","explanation":"Do something special.","explanation_ar":"نحتفل.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات الجديدة.","items":[{"q":"Write two sentences that show two meanings of *present*.","answer":"I got a present from my aunt. Everyone was present for the family photo.","explanation":"Two meanings.","explanation_ar":"معنيان.","dok":2,"skill":"multiple meanings","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write two sentences that show two meanings of *light*.","answer":"We turned on the light in the lantern. The gift box was light and easy to carry.","explanation":"Two meanings.","explanation_ar":"معنيان.","dok":2,"skill":"multiple meanings","stage":"production"},{"q":"Describe one Eid tradition in your family. Use *tradition* and *relatives*.","answer":"Our tradition is to visit all our relatives after the Eid prayer and eat ma'amoul together.","explanation":"Two words.","explanation_ar":"كلمتان.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why do you think hospitality is important in Saudi culture?","answer":"Because welcoming guests shows respect and kindness, and it brings people together.","explanation":"Reason.","explanation_ar":"سبب.","dok":3,"skill":"reasoning","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Two-Meaning Lanterns","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Lantern street: a sentence appears under a dark lantern; the child taps the meaning that fits. Correct answers make the lantern glow; when the street is lit, fireworks celebrate Eid.","Teams take turns picking a sentence card.","They read it aloud and point to the lantern word.","They choose the meaning card that fits the sentence.","Bonus: say a new sentence with the OTHER meaning.","Each correct meaning = 1 point; each bonus sentence = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *being friendly and generous to guests*?","answer":"hospitality","explanation":"Hospitality.","explanation_ar":"كرم الضيافة.","options":["hospitality","tradition","culture","symbol"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *multiple-meaning word*?","answer":"one word with two or more meanings","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"كلمة لها أكثر من معنى.","options":["one word with two or more meanings","two words that mean the same","a very long word","a word in two languages"],"dok":1,"skill":"multiple meanings"},{"q":"*Something people have done in the same way for many years* is a ___.","answer":"tradition","explanation":"Tradition.","explanation_ar":"تقليد.","options":["guest","tradition","feast","lantern"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*We heard the doorbell **ring**.* What does *ring* mean here?","answer":"make a bell sound","explanation":"Verb meaning.","explanation_ar":"يرنّ.","options":["jewelry","a circle","a box","make a bell sound"],"dok":1,"skill":"multiple meanings"},{"q":"Which sentence about the Eid guests is correct?","answer":"The guests were happy to see us.","explanation":"were.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The guests was happy to see us.","The guests is happy to see us.","The guests were happy to see us.","The guests be happy to see us."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *decorate* correctly?","answer":"We decorated the house with lights.","explanation":"decorate with.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We decorated the house by lights.","We decorate the house yesterday.","We decorating the house with lights.","We decorated the house with lights."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"*The **date** on my birthday card was wrong.* What does *date* mean here?","answer":"a day on the calendar","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"تاريخ.","options":["a sweet fruit","a friend","a day on the calendar","a palm tree"],"dok":2,"skill":"multiple meanings"},{"q":"Which is a *symbol*?","answer":"a palm tree that stands for Saudi Arabia","explanation":"Symbol.","explanation_ar":"رمز.","options":["a plate of rice","a palm tree that stands for Saudi Arabia","a long road","a cup of water"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is the BEST way to find the right meaning of a multiple-meaning word?","answer":"Try each meaning in the sentence and choose the one that makes sense.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"جرّب كل معنى في الجملة.","options":["Always choose the first meaning.","Ask what the word sounds like.","Try each meaning in the sentence and choose the one that makes sense.","Count its letters."],"dok":3,"skill":"strategy"},{"q":"*The guests brought a box of sweets and a **light** for the garden.* What does *light* mean here?","answer":"a lamp","explanation":"Context: for the garden.","explanation_ar":"مصباح.","options":["not heavy","a lamp","bright in color","early in the morning"],"dok":3,"skill":"multiple meanings"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'Eid': a family in new clothes greeting each other"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'Ramadan': a family at an iftar table at sunset"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'Founding Day': children in traditional clothes with a Founding Day banner"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'guest': a host welcoming a guest at the door"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'gift': a wrapped gift box"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'invite': a child giving an invitation card"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'decorate': children hanging lights and paper stars"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'lantern': a glowing brass lantern"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'feast': a big table full of food"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tradition': a grandfather giving coins to children"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'celebrate': a family celebrating with balloons and sweets"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'gather': many relatives in one room"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'relatives': a big family photo"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'hospitality': a host pouring Arabic coffee for guests"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'culture': Arabic coffee pot, dates and a traditional rug"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'unique': one red balloon among white balloons"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'symbol': the Saudi emblem with a palm tree and swords"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-01","prompt":"A family celebrating Eid at home."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-02","prompt":"Pictures of celebration words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS1-03","prompt":"Pictures showing two meanings of date, present and light."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V01","script":"Eid. We wear new clothes on Eid."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V02","script":"Ramadan. We break our fast together in Ramadan."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V03","script":"Founding Day. Our school celebrated Founding Day with a show."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V04","script":"guest. We served coffee to our guests."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V05","script":"gift. Grandma gave me a gift for Eid."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V06","script":"invite. We invited our neighbors to lunch."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V07","script":"decorate. We decorated the house with lights."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V08","script":"lantern. A lantern glowed by the door."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V09","script":"feast. The family had a feast after the Eid prayer."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V10","script":"tradition. Giving eidiya to children is an Eid tradition."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V11","script":"celebrate. We celebrate Eid with our relatives."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V12","script":"gather. The family gathered at Grandpa's house."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V13","script":"relatives. We visit our relatives on Eid."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V14","script":"hospitality. Saudi families are famous for their hospitality."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V15","script":"culture. Arabic coffee is part of our culture."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V16","script":"unique. Every country has unique Eid traditions."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-V17","script":"symbol. The palm tree is a symbol of Saudi Arabia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS1-VID","script":"Eid Mubarak! Today, we learn the words of celebrations and traditions. Ramadan, Eid and Founding Day! We invite guests, decorate the house, gather with relatives and share a feast! Traditions, gifts, lanterns and hospitality – they are part of our culture. Some words have two meanings! A date is a fruit… and a day on the calendar! Read the sentence, try each meaning and choose the one that fits!"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS2","title":"The Eid Lantern","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read a realistic story of about 290 words about kindness at Eid","map the plot on a Story Mountain: problem, rising action, turning point and resolution","explain how the main character changes from the beginning to the end"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة قصة واقعية من نحو 330 كلمة عن اللطف في العيد.","رسم الحبكة على جبل القصة: المشكلة والأحداث المتصاعدة ونقطة التحول والحل.","شرح كيف تتغير الشخصية الرئيسية من البداية إلى النهاية."],"success_criteria":["I can find the problem and the turning point of the story.","I can put the main events on the Story Mountain in order.","I can explain how Noura changes and why, with evidence."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع إيجاد مشكلة القصة ونقطة التحول.","أستطيع ترتيب الأحداث الرئيسية على جبل القصة.","أستطيع شرح كيف تتغير نورة ولماذا مع الدليل."],"map_area":"Literary Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"brass","pos":"noun","ipa":"/bræs/","definition":"a shiny yellow metal","arabic":"نحاس أصفر","example":"The old lantern was made of brass.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"wobble","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈwɑbəl/","definition":"to move a little from side to side, as if about to fall","arabic":"يتأرجح / يهتز","example":"The chair wobbled under her feet.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"crash","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kræʃ/","definition":"to fall and hit something hard and loudly","arabic":"يتحطم / يرتطم","example":"The lantern crashed onto the floor.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"ruined","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈruɪnd/","definition":"badly damaged or spoiled","arabic":"مُفسَد / مُدمَّر","example":"Noura thought Eid was ruined.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"elderly","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɛldərli/","definition":"old (a polite word for people)","arabic":"مُسنّ","example":"Mrs. Amal is our elderly neighbor.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"glow","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɡloʊ/","definition":"to give a soft, warm light","arabic":"يتوهّج","example":"The paper lantern glowed on the table.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"twist","pos":"verb","ipa":"/twɪst/","definition":"to turn or bend something around","arabic":"يلوي","example":"Faris twisted the wire into a handle.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"The **plot** is the chain of events in a story. Many stories climb a **Story Mountain**: **Beginning** (characters, setting) → **Problem** → **Rising action** (events that build up) → **Turning point** (the most important moment, when things start to change) → **Resolution** (how the problem is solved) → **Ending**.\n\nThe **turning point** is often a **decision** or a **new idea**. Look for words like *suddenly*, *had an idea* or *then*.\n\nCharacters often **change** from the beginning to the end. Ask: **How does the character feel or think at the start? At the end? What made her change?** Give evidence from both parts.","examples":["**Beginning:** *On the last night of Ramadan, Noura climbed onto a chair to hang the old brass lantern.*","**Problem:** *The lantern slipped from her hands and crashed onto the floor.*","**Noura's feeling at the start:** *\"Now Eid is ruined.\"* – sad, thinking about the lantern.","**Rising action:** *Mother hugged her. \"Eid is about people, not things.\"* / Noura sees Mrs. Amal's dark house.","**Turning point:** *Suddenly, Noura had an idea.*","**More action:** she makes a lantern from a jar; they knock on Mrs. Amal's door and invite her.","**Resolution:** *Mrs. Amal sat at Noura's table, laughing and sharing ma'amoul.*","**Ending:** *The broken brass lantern still sat in the corner, but nobody noticed it at all.*","**How Noura changes:** from sad about a **thing** to happy about **people** – because of Mother's words and seeing Mrs. Amal alone."],"common_errors":["✗ The turning point is when the lantern breaks. → ✓ That is the problem; the turning point is Noura's idea. (Problem ≠ turning point.)","✗ Listing every small event. → ✓ Choose the main events for the mountain. (Main events only.)","✗ Noura is sad. (with no change) → ✓ Noura is sad at first, but happy and kind at the end. (Show the **change**.)","✗ The lantern crash on the floor. → ✓ The lantern crashed onto the floor. (Past tense.)","✗ Mrs. Amal live alone. → ✓ Mrs. Amal lived alone. (Past tense in the story.)","✗ Faris helped her to twisted the wire. → ✓ Faris helped her twist the wire. (help + verb.)"],"arabic":"الحبكة سلسلة أحداث القصة، وكثير من القصص تصعد «جبل القصة»: البداية ← المشكلة ← الأحداث المتصاعدة ← نقطة التحول ← الحل ← النهاية.\n\nنقطة التحول غالبًا قرار أو فكرة جديدة، وابحث عن كلمات مثل suddenly وhad an idea.\n\nالشخصيات تتغير غالبًا: كيف كانت تشعر في البداية؟ وفي النهاية؟ وما الذي غيّرها؟ قدّم دليلًا من الجزأين.\n\nتغيّرت نورة من الحزن على شيء (الفانوس) إلى الفرح بالناس، بسبب كلام أمها ورؤيتها جارتها وحيدة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"It's the last night of Ramadan. Tomorrow is Eid! But something is about to go wrong…","on_screen":"The Eid Lantern 🏮","visual":"Noura on a chair holding a brass lantern by the door."},{"time":"00:20–01:50","narration":"On the last night of Ramadan, Noura climbed onto a chair…","on_screen":"(story)","visual":"Animated story: the crash, Mother's hug, the dark house across the street, the jar lantern, the knock on the door, the Eid breakfast."},{"time":"01:50–02:25","narration":"Climb the Story Mountain: problem, rising action, turning point, resolution!","on_screen":"Story Mountain ⛰️","visual":"A mountain path lights up as each event is placed on it."},{"time":"02:25–02:45","narration":"How did Noura change? From sad about a thing to happy about people!","on_screen":"Character change","visual":"Two pictures of Noura: crying by the broken lantern, then smiling with Mrs. Amal."},{"time":"02:45–03:00","narration":"Eid is about people, not things.","on_screen":"Eid Mubarak! 🌙","visual":"The paper lantern glows on the breakfast table."}],"captions_ar":["إنها الليلة الأخيرة من رمضان، وغدًا العيد! لكن شيئًا ما سيحدث…","في الليلة الأخيرة من رمضان صعدت نورة على كرسي…","اصعد جبل القصة: المشكلة، الأحداث المتصاعدة، نقطة التحول، الحل!","كيف تغيّرت نورة؟ من الحزن على شيء إلى الفرح بالناس!","العيد للناس لا للأشياء."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, gentle female storyteller, speed 0.90. Different voices for Noura, Mother and Mrs. Amal."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Eid Lantern","body":"On the last night of Ramadan, Noura climbed onto a chair to hang the old brass lantern by the front door. Grandpa had bought it in the souq many years ago, and every year it glowed for Eid.\n\nSuddenly, the chair wobbled. The lantern slipped from her hands and crashed onto the floor. Its little glass window cracked into pieces.\n\n\"Oh no!\" Noura cried. Tears filled her eyes. \"Now Eid is ruined.\"\n\nMother hugged her. \"A lantern is just a lantern,\" she said gently. \"Eid is about people, not things.\"\n\nNoura went to her bedroom window to think. Across the street, the house of their elderly neighbor, Mrs. Amal, was completely dark. Mrs. Amal lived alone, and her children lived far away in another city.\n\nSuddenly, Noura had an idea. She found an empty glass jar, colored paper, glue and a short piece of wire. For an hour, she cut out stars and moons and stuck them onto the jar. Faris helped her twist the wire into a handle, and Mother put a small battery light inside.\n\nThen they crossed the street and knocked on Mrs. Amal's door.\n\n\"Eid Mubarak!\" said Noura, holding out the glowing jar. \"We made you an Eid lantern. And we'd like to invite you to breakfast with us tomorrow.\"\n\nMrs. Amal's eyes shone brighter than the lantern. \"My dear,\" she said softly, \"this is the best gift I've had in years.\"\n\nThe next morning, after the Eid prayer, Mrs. Amal sat at Noura's table, laughing and sharing ma'amoul with the family. She had brought her paper lantern with her, and it glowed in the middle of the table. The broken brass lantern still sat in the corner, but nobody noticed it at all.","word_count":288,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L04-U6-LS2-1","genre":"narrative","role":"main","difficulty":"450L–600L","glossary":[{"word":"brass","definition":"a shiny yellow metal","arabic":"نحاس أصفر"},{"word":"wobble","definition":"to move a little from side to side, as if about to fall","arabic":"يتأرجح / يهتز"},{"word":"ma'amoul","definition":"small cookies filled with dates or nuts, made for Eid","arabic":"معمول"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the story. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ القصة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"When does the story begin?","answer":"on the last night of Ramadan","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"في الليلة الأخيرة من رمضان.","options":["on Founding Day","on the first day of school","on a summer morning","on the last night of Ramadan"],"dok":1,"skill":"setting","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who bought the brass lantern?","answer":"Grandpa","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الجد.","options":["Grandpa","Mother","Mrs. Amal","Faris"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did the lantern fall?","answer":"The chair wobbled, and it slipped from Noura's hands.","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"اهتز الكرسي فانزلق الفانوس.","options":["Faris pushed it.","The wind blew it down.","Mrs. Amal knocked on the door.","The chair wobbled, and it slipped from Noura's hands."],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why was Mrs. Amal's house dark?","answer":"She lived alone, and her children lived far away.","explanation":"Paragraph 5.","explanation_ar":"كانت تعيش وحدها.","options":["There was a sandstorm.","She was at the souq.","She lived alone, and her children lived far away.","Her lantern was broken too."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Noura use to make the new lantern?","answer":"an empty glass jar, colored paper, glue and wire","explanation":"Paragraph 6.","explanation_ar":"برطمان وورق ملوّن وغراء وسلك.","options":["an empty glass jar, colored paper, glue and wire","a brass box and glass","wood and nails","a plastic bottle and sand"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Noura invite Mrs. Amal to?","answer":"breakfast with the family the next morning","explanation":"Paragraph 8.","explanation_ar":"فطور العيد.","options":["a trip to the souq","breakfast with the family the next morning","a school show","a football match"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plot and character change. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الحبكة وتغيّر الشخصية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What is the PROBLEM in *The Eid Lantern*?","answer":"The old brass lantern breaks.","explanation":"Problem.","explanation_ar":"انكسار الفانوس.","options":["Mrs. Amal comes to breakfast.","Noura makes a jar lantern.","The old brass lantern breaks.","Faris twists the wire."],"dok":1,"skill":"plot","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What is the TURNING POINT of the story?","answer":"Noura has an idea after seeing Mrs. Amal's dark house.","explanation":"Turning point.","explanation_ar":"فكرة نورة.","options":["The chair wobbles.","Noura has an idea after seeing Mrs. Amal's dark house.","Grandpa buys the lantern.","Mrs. Amal eats ma'amoul."],"dok":2,"skill":"plot","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which event is part of the RISING ACTION?","answer":"Mother tells Noura that Eid is about people, not things.","explanation":"Builds up.","explanation_ar":"حدث متصاعد.","options":["The family eats breakfast with Mrs. Amal.","Mother tells Noura that Eid is about people, not things.","Nobody notices the broken lantern.","Grandpa bought the lantern years ago."],"dok":2,"skill":"plot","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How does Noura feel at the BEGINNING of the story?","answer":"sad and upset about the broken lantern","explanation":"\"Now Eid is ruined.\"","explanation_ar":"حزينة.","options":["excited about breakfast","angry with Mrs. Amal","bored","sad and upset about the broken lantern"],"dok":1,"skill":"character feelings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What helped Noura change?","answer":"her mother's words and seeing her lonely neighbor's dark house","explanation":"Cause of change.","explanation_ar":"كلام أمها ورؤية جارتها.","options":["a new brass lantern from the souq","a gift from Grandpa","her mother's words and seeing her lonely neighbor's dark house","a phone call from her friend"],"dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does *nobody noticed it at all* at the end show?","answer":"The family was happy with people, not thinking about the broken lantern.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"انشغلوا بالناس عن الفانوس.","options":["The family was happy with people, not thinking about the broken lantern.","The lantern was hidden.","Everyone was asleep.","The brass lantern was fixed."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence that shows the turning point.","answer":"Suddenly, Noura had an idea.","explanation":"Turning point.","explanation_ar":"نقطة التحول.","evidence":"Suddenly, Noura had an idea.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L04-U6-LS2-1","dok":1,"skill":"plot","stage":"freer","sentences":["On the last night of Ramadan, Noura climbed onto a chair to hang the old brass lantern by the front door.","Grandpa had bought it in the souq many years ago, and every year it glowed for Eid.","Suddenly, the chair wobbled.","The lantern slipped from her hands and crashed onto the floor.","Its little glass window cracked into pieces.","\"Oh no!\"","Noura cried.","Tears filled her eyes.","\"Now Eid is ruined.\"","Mother hugged her.","\"A lantern is just a lantern,\" she said gently.","\"Eid is about people, not things.\"","Noura went to her bedroom window to think.","Across the street, the house of their elderly neighbor, Mrs.","Amal, was completely dark.","Mrs.","Amal lived alone, and her children lived far away in another city.","Suddenly, Noura had an idea.","She found an empty glass jar, colored paper, glue and a short piece of wire.","For an hour, she cut out stars and moons and stuck them onto the jar.","Faris helped her twist the wire into a handle, and Mother put a small battery light inside.","Then they crossed the street and knocked on Mrs.","Amal's door.","\"Eid Mubarak!\" said Noura, holding out the glowing jar.","\"We made you an Eid lantern.","And we'd like to invite you to breakfast with us tomorrow.\"","Mrs.","Amal's eyes shone brighter than the lantern.","\"My dear,\" she said softly, \"this is the best gift I've had in years.\"","The next morning, after the Eid prayer, Mrs.","Amal sat at Noura's table, laughing and sharing ma'amoul with the family.","She had brought her paper lantern with her, and it glowed in the middle of the table.","The broken brass lantern still sat in the corner, but nobody noticed it at all."]},{"q":"Underline the words that show how Mrs. Amal felt when she got the lantern.","answer":"Mrs. Amal's eyes shone brighter than the lantern.","explanation":"Feelings clue.","explanation_ar":"دليل الشعور.","evidence":"Mrs. Amal's eyes shone brighter than the lantern.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L04-U6-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["On the last night of Ramadan, Noura climbed onto a chair to hang the old brass lantern by the front door.","Grandpa had bought it in the souq many years ago, and every year it glowed for Eid.","Suddenly, the chair wobbled.","The lantern slipped from her hands and crashed onto the floor.","Its little glass window cracked into pieces.","\"Oh no!\"","Noura cried.","Tears filled her eyes.","\"Now Eid is ruined.\"","Mother hugged her.","\"A lantern is just a lantern,\" she said gently.","\"Eid is about people, not things.\"","Noura went to her bedroom window to think.","Across the street, the house of their elderly neighbor, Mrs.","Amal, was completely dark.","Mrs.","Amal lived alone, and her children lived far away in another city.","Suddenly, Noura had an idea.","She found an empty glass jar, colored paper, glue and a short piece of wire.","For an hour, she cut out stars and moons and stuck them onto the jar.","Faris helped her twist the wire into a handle, and Mother put a small battery light inside.","Then they crossed the street and knocked on Mrs.","Amal's door.","\"Eid Mubarak!\" said Noura, holding out the glowing jar.","\"We made you an Eid lantern.","And we'd like to invite you to breakfast with us tomorrow.\"","Mrs.","Amal's eyes shone brighter than the lantern.","\"My dear,\" she said softly, \"this is the best gift I've had in years.\"","The next morning, after the Eid prayer, Mrs.","Amal sat at Noura's table, laughing and sharing ma'amoul with the family.","She had brought her paper lantern with her, and it glowed in the middle of the table.","The broken brass lantern still sat in the corner, but nobody noticed it at all."]},{"q":"Which sentence BEST states the theme of *The Eid Lantern*?","answer":"Eid is about people, not things.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"العيد للناس لا للأشياء.","options":["Eid is about people, not things.","Brass lanterns are expensive.","Chairs can be dangerous.","Ma'amoul is delicious."],"dok":2,"skill":"theme","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Why does the writer say Mrs. Amal's eyes *shone brighter than the lantern*?","answer":"to show how happy and touched she was","explanation":"Figurative language.","explanation_ar":"ليُظهر شدة فرحها.","options":["to say her eyes were lights","to describe the weather","to show she was tired","to show how happy and touched she was"],"dok":3,"skill":"figurative language","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain the plot and the change.","instructions_ar":"اشرح الحبكة والتغيّر.","items":[{"q":"Write the problem and the resolution of the story.","answer":"Problem: Noura breaks the old brass lantern. Resolution: she makes a new lantern for Mrs. Amal, and they celebrate Eid together.","explanation":"Problem + resolution.","explanation_ar":"المشكلة + الحل.","dok":2,"skill":"plot","stage":"production"},{"q":"How does Noura change? Give evidence from the beginning and the end.","answer":"At the beginning, she is sad: 'Now Eid is ruined.' At the end, she is happy sharing breakfast with Mrs. Amal.","explanation":"Change + evidence.","explanation_ar":"التغيّر + الدليل.","dok":3,"skill":"character change","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why do you think Mrs. Amal said it was *the best gift I've had in years*?","answer":"Because she was lonely, and the lantern showed that her neighbors cared about her.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"استنتاج.","dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"production"},{"q":"Who could YOU invite or help this Eid? How?","answer":"I could visit my grandfather's old friend and take him a plate of ma'amoul.","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"ربط.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"The Eid Lantern\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “The Eid Lantern”.","answer":"288 words","title":"The Eid Lantern","body":"On the last night of Ramadan, Noura climbed onto a chair to hang the old brass lantern by the front door. Grandpa had bought it in the souq many years ago, and every year it glowed for Eid.\n\nSuddenly, the chair wobbled. The lantern slipped from her hands and crashed onto the floor. Its little glass window cracked into pieces.\n\n\"Oh no!\" Noura cried. Tears filled her eyes. \"Now Eid is ruined.\"\n\nMother hugged her. \"A lantern is just a lantern,\" she said gently. \"Eid is about people, not things.\"\n\nNoura went to her bedroom window to think. Across the street, the house of their elderly neighbor, Mrs. Amal, was completely dark. Mrs. Amal lived alone, and her children lived far away in another city.\n\nSuddenly, Noura had an idea. She found an empty glass jar, colored paper, glue and a short piece of wire. For an hour, she cut out stars and moons and stuck them onto the jar. Faris helped her twist the wire into a handle, and Mother put a small battery light inside.\n\nThen they crossed the street and knocked on Mrs. Amal's door.\n\n\"Eid Mubarak!\" said Noura, holding out the glowing jar. \"We made you an Eid lantern. And we'd like to invite you to breakfast with us tomorrow.\"\n\nMrs. Amal's eyes shone brighter than the lantern. \"My dear,\" she said softly, \"this is the best gift I've had in years.\"\n\nThe next morning, after the Eid prayer, Mrs. Amal sat at Noura's table, laughing and sharing ma'amoul with the family. She had brought her paper lantern with her, and it glowed in the middle of the table. The broken brass lantern still sat in the corner, but nobody noticed it at all.","words":288,"limit":218}],"time_limit_sec":218}],"game":{"name":"Climb the Story Mountain","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Mountain climb: the child drags event cards onto the mountain path in order; correct placements move a lantern up the mountain. At the turning point, the child must choose Noura's new feeling to continue to the resolution.","Teams get the event cards in a mixed pile.","They place the cards on the mountain: beginning, problem, rising action, turning point, resolution, ending.","For the beginning and the ending, they add a feelings card for Noura.","The teacher checks; the team explains the turning point.","Each correct card = 1 point; correct turning point + explanation = 3 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Who is the main character of *The Eid Lantern*?","answer":"Noura","explanation":"Main character.","explanation_ar":"نورة.","options":["Mrs. Amal","Mother","Noura","Faris"],"dok":1,"skill":"characters"},{"q":"*The chair **wobbled**.* *Wobbled* means ___.","answer":"moved a little from side to side","explanation":"Wobble.","explanation_ar":"اهتز.","options":["stayed very still","flew away","moved a little from side to side","got bigger"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word describes Mrs. Amal in a polite way?","answer":"elderly","explanation":"Elderly.","explanation_ar":"مسنّة.","options":["elderly","brass","ruined","light"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is the *turning point* of a story?","answer":"the important moment when things start to change","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"لحظة التحول.","options":["the first sentence","the title","the important moment when things start to change","the place"],"dok":1,"skill":"plot"},{"q":"Which sentence about the lantern is correct?","answer":"The lantern crashed onto the floor.","explanation":"Past tense.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The lantern crashed onto the floor.","The lantern crash onto the floor yesterday.","The lantern crashing onto the floor.","The lantern crashes onto the floor last night."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence about Faris is correct?","answer":"Faris helped Noura twist the wire.","explanation":"help + verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Faris helped Noura to twisted the wire.","Faris help Noura twisting the wire.","Faris helped Noura twists the wire.","Faris helped Noura twist the wire."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What did Mother mean by *A lantern is just a lantern*?","answer":"Things are less important than people.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"الأشياء أقل أهمية من الناس.","options":["Things are less important than people.","Lanterns are cheap.","She doesn't like lanterns.","She will buy a new lantern."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"Which event is the RESOLUTION?","answer":"Mrs. Amal shares Eid breakfast with Noura's family.","explanation":"Resolution.","explanation_ar":"الحل.","options":["The chair wobbles.","Noura climbs onto a chair.","Grandpa buys the lantern.","Mrs. Amal shares Eid breakfast with Noura's family."],"dok":2,"skill":"plot"},{"q":"How did Noura's kindness change Mrs. Amal's Eid?","answer":"She was alone and in the dark, but now she celebrated with a family.","explanation":"Character change.","explanation_ar":"لم تعد وحيدة.","options":["She went to another city.","She was alone and in the dark, but now she celebrated with a family.","She bought a brass lantern.","She slept all day."],"dok":3,"skill":"character change"},{"q":"Which saying BEST matches the story?","answer":"Happiness grows when you share it.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"السعادة تكبر حين نتشاركها.","options":["Look before you leap.","Happiness grows when you share it.","The early bird catches the worm.","Practice makes perfect."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'brass': a shiny brass lantern"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'wobble': a wobbly chair"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'crash': a lantern hitting the floor"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ruined': a broken cake on the floor"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'elderly': an elderly woman smiling at her door"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'glow': a lantern glowing softly"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'twist': hands twisting a wire"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-01","prompt":"Noura sad next to a broken lantern, with Mother hugging her."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-02","prompt":"Noura giving a jar lantern to her elderly neighbor."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS2-03","prompt":"A Story Mountain plot diagram."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V01","script":"brass. The old lantern was made of brass."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V02","script":"wobble. The chair wobbled under her feet."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V03","script":"crash. The lantern crashed onto the floor."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V04","script":"ruined. Noura thought Eid was ruined."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V05","script":"elderly. Mrs. Amal is our elderly neighbor."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V06","script":"glow. The paper lantern glowed on the table."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-V07","script":"twist. Faris twisted the wire into a handle."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-T1","script":"On the last night of Ramadan, Noura climbed onto a chair to hang the old brass lantern by the front door. Grandpa had bought it in the souq many years ago, and every year it glowed for Eid. Suddenly, the chair wobbled. The lantern slipped from her hands and crashed onto the floor. Its little glass window cracked into pieces. \"Oh no!\" Noura cried. Tears filled her eyes. \"Now Eid is ruined.\" Mother hugged her. \"A lantern is just a lantern,\" she said gently. \"Eid is about people, not things.\" Noura went to her bedroom window to think. Across the street, the house of their elderly neighbor, Mrs. Amal, was completely dark. Mrs. Amal lived alone, and her children lived far away in another city. Suddenly, Noura had an idea. She found an empty glass jar, colored paper, glue and a short piece of wire. For an hour, she cut out stars and moons and stuck them onto the jar. Faris helped her twist the wire into a handle, and Mother put a small battery light inside. Then they crossed the street and knocked on Mrs. Amal's door. \"Eid Mubarak!\" said Noura, holding out the glowing jar. \"We made you an Eid lantern. And we'd like to invite you to breakfast with us tomorrow.\" Mrs. Amal's eyes shone brighter than the lantern. \"My dear,\" she said softly, \"this is the best gift I've had in years.\" The next morning, after the Eid prayer, Mrs. Amal sat at Noura's table, laughing and sharing ma'amoul with the family. She had brought her paper lantern with her, and it glowed in the middle of the table. The broken brass lantern still sat in the corner, but nobody noticed it at all."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS2-VID","script":"It's the last night of Ramadan. Tomorrow is Eid! But something is about to go wrong… On the last night of Ramadan, Noura climbed onto a chair… Climb the Story Mountain: problem, rising action, turning point, resolution! How did Noura change? From sad about a thing to happy about people! Eid is about people, not things."}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS3","title":"Compare a story and an article on one topic: \"Eid Around the World\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["compare a story and an article on the same topic (Eid)","explain what each text gives the reader: feelings and a lesson (story) or facts (article)","complete a Venn diagram and say what I learned from reading both"],"objectives_ar":["مقارنة قصة ومقال عن الموضوع نفسه (العيد).","شرح ما يقدمه كل نص للقارئ: مشاعر ودرس (القصة) أو حقائق (المقال).","إكمال مخطط فن وقول ما تعلمته من قراءة النصين."],"success_criteria":["I can find ideas that are only in the story, only in the article and in both.","I can explain how a story and an article are written differently.","I can say why reading both texts helps me understand the topic better."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع إيجاد أفكار في القصة فقط وفي المقال فقط وفي الاثنين.","أستطيع شرح اختلاف طريقة كتابة القصة والمقال.","أستطيع قول لماذا تساعدني قراءة النصين على فهم الموضوع أفضل."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"eidiya","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈidiə/","definition":"a gift of money that adults give children at Eid","arabic":"العيدية","example":"Grandpa gave us eidiya after the prayer.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"village","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɪlɪdʒ/","definition":"a small town in the countryside","arabic":"قرية","example":"They travel back to their home village.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"woven","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈwoʊvən/","definition":"made by crossing strips over and under each other","arabic":"منسوج","example":"Ketupat is cooked in woven palm leaves.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"forehead","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfɔrhɛd/","definition":"the part of your face above your eyes","arabic":"الجبهة","example":"He touched the hand to his forehead.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"henna","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɛnə/","definition":"a plant paste used to make red-brown patterns on hands","arabic":"حناء","example":"Girls decorate their hands with henna.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"bangle","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbæŋɡəl/","definition":"a round bracelet","arabic":"سوار","example":"She bought colorful bangles for Eid.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"although","pos":"conjunction","ipa":"/ɔlˈðoʊ/","definition":"used to show that two ideas are different","arabic":"مع أنّ","example":"Although the traditions are different, the meaning is the same.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **story** and an **article** can be about the **same topic** but give the reader **different things**. A **story** has **characters**, a **problem** and **feelings**, and it teaches a **lesson**. An **article** gives **facts**, often with **headings**, and teaches **information**.\n\nTo compare, ask: **What is only in the story? What is only in the article? What is in both?** Use a **Venn diagram**: two circles that overlap in the middle.\n\nThen ask: **What do I understand better because I read both?** The article tells us **what** people do at Eid; the story shows us **how it feels** and **why** it matters.","examples":["**Topic of both:** Eid al-Fitr at the end of Ramadan.","**Only the story:** characters (Noura, Mrs. Amal), a problem (the broken lantern), feelings (sad → happy).","**Only the article:** facts about five countries, headings, special words (Lebaran, ketupat, Chand Raat).","**Both:** Eid is about **people** and **sharing food** – *ma'amoul* appears in both texts!","**Different structure:** the story goes in **time order** (beginning → end); the article is organized by **country**.","**Different purpose:** the story **entertains** and teaches a lesson; the article **informs**.","**Same message:** story – *Eid is about people, not things.* / article – *spending time with the people we love.*","Compare with *both … and*: ***Both texts show that Eid is about sharing.***","Contrast with *although / but / however*: ***Although the story has one family, the article describes many countries.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Saying they are the same because both are about Eid. → ✓ Also say how they are different. (Compare AND contrast.)","✗ Putting a story detail in 'both'. → ✓ Check: is it in BOTH texts? (Both = in the two texts.)","✗ The article has characters. → ✓ The article has facts about people in many countries. (Articles give facts, not characters.)","✗ Both texts is about Eid. → ✓ Both texts are about Eid. (Both → **are**.)","✗ Although the story is short, but it is sad. → ✓ Although the story is short, it is sad. (Don't use **although** and **but** together.)","✗ In Turkey, children kisses the hands. → ✓ In Turkey, children kiss the hands. (Children (plural) → **kiss**.)"],"arabic":"قد تتناول القصة والمقال الموضوع نفسه لكن كلًّا منهما يقدم شيئًا مختلفًا: القصة شخصيات ومشكلة ومشاعر ودرس، والمقال حقائق وعناوين ومعلومات.\n\nللمقارنة اسأل: ما الموجود في القصة فقط؟ وفي المقال فقط؟ وفي الاثنين؟ واستخدم مخطط فن (دائرتان متداخلتان).\n\nثم اسأل: ماذا فهمت أفضل لأنني قرأت النصين؟ المقال يخبرنا ماذا يفعل الناس، والقصة تُرينا الشعور ولماذا يهم الأمر.\n\nلا تجمع although وbut في جملة واحدة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Eid Mubarak from around the world! Let's visit five countries in three minutes!","on_screen":"Eid Around the World 🌍","visual":"A spinning globe with lanterns lighting up in different countries."},{"time":"00:20–01:30","narration":"More than a billion Muslims celebrate Eid al-Fitr…","on_screen":"(article, country by country)","visual":"Saudi family with eidiya; Indonesian travelers and ketupat; Turkish children kissing elders' hands; Pakistani henna and bangles; prayer in a UK park."},{"time":"01:30–02:10","narration":"Story or article? The story has characters and feelings; the article has facts and headings.","on_screen":"Story vs. Article","visual":"Two books open: one with Noura's picture, one with a map."},{"time":"02:10–02:40","narration":"Venn diagram: only the story, only the article – and both in the middle!","on_screen":"Venn diagram ⭕⭕","visual":"Two circles overlap; 'Eid is about people' moves to the middle."},{"time":"02:40–03:00","narration":"Different traditions, one message: share and spend time with the people you love.","on_screen":"One message ❤️","visual":"Children from five countries wave together."}],"captions_ar":["عيد مبارك من حول العالم! لنزر خمس دول في ثلاث دقائق!","يحتفل أكثر من مليار مسلم بعيد الفطر…","قصة أم مقال؟ القصة فيها شخصيات ومشاعر، والمقال فيه حقائق وعناوين.","مخطط فن: القصة فقط، المقال فقط، والاثنان في الوسط!","تقاليد مختلفة ورسالة واحدة: تشاركوا وامضوا الوقت مع من تحبون."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly male presenter, speed 0.90. Noura reads parts of the article."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Eid Around the World","body":"More than a billion Muslims celebrate Eid al-Fitr at the end of Ramadan. Everyone says \"Eid Mubarak,\" but each country has its own unique traditions.\n\n**Saudi Arabia** – After the Eid prayer, families visit their relatives, drink Arabic coffee and eat ma'amoul. Children receive eidiya, gifts of money from adults.\n\n**Indonesia** – In Indonesia, Eid is called Lebaran. Millions of people travel back to their home villages to celebrate with their families. A favorite food is ketupat, rice cooked inside little baskets of woven palm leaves.\n\n**Turkey** – Children kiss the hands of older relatives and touch them to their foreheads to show respect. In return, they get sweets or a little money, so some people call Eid the \"Sugar Festival.\"\n\n**Pakistan** – On Chand Raat, the night before Eid, girls and women decorate their hands with henna and shop for colorful bangles.\n\n**The United Kingdom** – Thousands of Muslims gather in big parks to pray together in the open air.\n\nAlthough the traditions are different, Eid everywhere is about the same things: thanking Allah, sharing food and spending time with the people we love.","word_count":180,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L04-U6-LS3-1","genre":"informational","role":"paired","difficulty":"450L–600L","glossary":[{"word":"eidiya","definition":"a gift of money that adults give children at Eid","arabic":"العيدية"},{"word":"henna","definition":"a plant paste used to make red-brown patterns on hands","arabic":"حناء"},{"word":"bangles","definition":"round bracelets","arabic":"أساور"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read *Eid Around the World*. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقال واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"When do Muslims celebrate Eid al-Fitr?","answer":"at the end of Ramadan","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"في نهاية رمضان.","options":["at the start of Ramadan","on Founding Day","at the end of Ramadan","in the middle of summer only"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","text_id":"TXT-RW-L04-U6-LS3-1","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is Eid called in Indonesia?","answer":"Lebaran","explanation":"Indonesia.","explanation_ar":"ليباران.","options":["Chand Raat","the Sugar Festival","Eidiya","Lebaran"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is *ketupat*?","answer":"rice cooked inside little baskets of woven palm leaves","explanation":"Indonesia.","explanation_ar":"أرز مطبوخ في سلال من سعف النخيل.","options":["a kind of henna","rice cooked inside little baskets of woven palm leaves","a sweet drink","a bracelet"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why do some people in Turkey call Eid the *Sugar Festival*?","answer":"Children get sweets when they show respect to older relatives.","explanation":"Turkey: so → effect.","explanation_ar":"لأن الأطفال يحصلون على الحلوى.","options":["People only eat sugar on Eid.","Children get sweets when they show respect to older relatives.","Turkey makes the most sugar.","It is always sweet weather."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What happens on Chand Raat in Pakistan?","answer":"Girls and women decorate their hands with henna and shop for bangles.","explanation":"Pakistan.","explanation_ar":"الحناء والأساور.","options":["People travel to villages.","Families pray in parks.","Children get eidiya.","Girls and women decorate their hands with henna and shop for bangles."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where do many Muslims in the United Kingdom pray on Eid?","answer":"in big parks in the open air","explanation":"The United Kingdom.","explanation_ar":"في الحدائق الكبيرة.","options":["in big parks in the open air","on boats","only at home","in shopping malls"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Compare the story and the article. Choose.","instructions_ar":"قارن القصة والمقال: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What do *The Eid Lantern* and *Eid Around the World* have in common?","answer":"Both are about Eid al-Fitr.","explanation":"Same topic.","explanation_ar":"الموضوع نفسه.","options":["Both have headings.","Both are about Indonesia.","Both are about Eid al-Fitr.","Both have a broken lantern."],"dok":1,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is found ONLY in the story?","answer":"a character who changes from sad to happy","explanation":"Story feature.","explanation_ar":"شخصية تتغير.","options":["facts about five countries","headings for each country","a character who changes from sad to happy","the word Lebaran"],"dok":1,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is found ONLY in the article?","answer":"facts about Eid in Turkey and Pakistan","explanation":"Article feature.","explanation_ar":"حقائق عن تركيا وباكستان.","options":["Noura's idea","Mrs. Amal's dark house","a problem and a resolution","facts about Eid in Turkey and Pakistan"],"dok":1,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which food appears in BOTH texts?","answer":"ma'amoul","explanation":"Both.","explanation_ar":"المعمول.","options":["ma'amoul","ketupat","kahk","pizza"],"dok":2,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How is the article organized?","answer":"by country, with a heading for each","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"حسب الدولة.","options":["by country, with a heading for each","in time order from beginning to end","as a play with stage directions","as numbered steps"],"dok":2,"skill":"text structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What do you understand BETTER because you read both texts?","answer":"Eid traditions are different, but the meaning – sharing with people we love – is the same.","explanation":"Synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التقاليد تختلف والمعنى واحد.","options":["Brass lanterns break easily.","Eid traditions are different, but the meaning – sharing with people we love – is the same.","Indonesia has many villages.","Henna is red-brown."],"dok":3,"skill":"synthesis","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Venn diagram.","instructions_ar":"أكمل مخطط فن.","items":[{"q":"Only in the story – write three ideas.","answer":"Noura and Mrs. Amal; the broken lantern problem; Noura's feelings change from sad to happy.","explanation":"Story only.","explanation_ar":"القصة فقط.","dok":1,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"freer"},{"q":"In both texts – write two ideas.","answer":"Both are about Eid al-Fitr. Both show that Eid is about sharing food and spending time with people.","explanation":"Both.","explanation_ar":"الاثنان.","dok":2,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Only in the article – write three ideas.","answer":"facts about five countries; headings; Lebaran, ketupat, henna and the Sugar Festival","explanation":"Article only.","explanation_ar":"المقال فقط.","dok":1,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"freer"},{"q":"What did you learn from reading both?","answer":"I learned that Eid looks different around the world, but everywhere it brings people together.","explanation":"Synthesis.","explanation_ar":"الخلاصة.","dok":3,"skill":"synthesis","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Compare and connect.","instructions_ar":"قارن واربط.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with *both* to compare the two texts.","answer":"Both texts show that Eid is a time to share food with others.","explanation":"both.","explanation_ar":"both.","dok":2,"skill":"compare sentences","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a sentence with *although* to contrast the two texts.","answer":"Although the story is about one family, the article describes Eid in five countries.","explanation":"although.","explanation_ar":"although.","dok":2,"skill":"contrast sentences","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which tradition from the article would Noura like? Why?","answer":"The Turkish tradition of showing respect to older relatives, because she cares about Mrs. Amal.","explanation":"Connect texts.","explanation_ar":"ربط النصين.","dok":3,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which tradition from your family could you add to the article? Write it like the article.","answer":"Saudi Arabia – In our family, we gather at Grandpa's house and the youngest child pours the Arabic coffee for the guests.","explanation":"Write like the article.","explanation_ar":"اكتب بأسلوب المقال.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Eid Around the World\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Eid Around the World”.","answer":"180 words","title":"Eid Around the World","body":"More than a billion Muslims celebrate Eid al-Fitr at the end of Ramadan. Everyone says \"Eid Mubarak,\" but each country has its own unique traditions.\n\n**Saudi Arabia** – After the Eid prayer, families visit their relatives, drink Arabic coffee and eat ma'amoul. Children receive eidiya, gifts of money from adults.\n\n**Indonesia** – In Indonesia, Eid is called Lebaran. Millions of people travel back to their home villages to celebrate with their families. A favorite food is ketupat, rice cooked inside little baskets of woven palm leaves.\n\n**Turkey** – Children kiss the hands of older relatives and touch them to their foreheads to show respect. In return, they get sweets or a little money, so some people call Eid the \"Sugar Festival.\"\n\n**Pakistan** – On Chand Raat, the night before Eid, girls and women decorate their hands with henna and shop for colorful bangles.\n\n**The United Kingdom** – Thousands of Muslims gather in big parks to pray together in the open air.\n\nAlthough the traditions are different, Eid everywhere is about the same things: thanking Allah, sharing food and spending time with the people we love.","words":180,"limit":142}],"time_limit_sec":142},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Venn Diagram","instructions":"Organizer: Write ideas that are only in the story, only in the article and in both texts. Then write what you learned from reading both.","items":[{"q":"Only in the story (The Eid Lantern)","answer":"characters (Noura, Mrs. Amal); a problem (the broken lantern); feelings change from sad to happy; time order"},{"q":"In both texts","answer":"the topic of Eid al-Fitr; sharing food (ma'amoul); Eid is about people we love"},{"q":"Only in the article (Eid Around the World)","answer":"facts about five countries; headings; Lebaran, ketupat, henna, the Sugar Festival; organized by country"},{"q":"What I learned from reading both","answer":"Eid traditions are different around the world, but the meaning is the same: sharing and caring for people"}]}],"game":{"name":"Venn Lantern Sort","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Lantern circles: idea lanterns float onto the screen; the child drags each one into the story circle, the article circle or the middle. Correct lanterns light up; a full, correct diagram sets off Eid fireworks.","Teams get a pile of idea cards.","They take turns placing cards in the story circle, the article circle or the middle (both).","The team must explain each 'both' card.","The other teams check.","Each correct card = 1 point; each correct 'both' explanation = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *Venn diagram*?","answer":"two circles that overlap to compare two things","explanation":"Venn diagram.","explanation_ar":"دائرتان متداخلتان للمقارنة.","options":["a list of steps","two circles that overlap to compare two things","a map","a timeline"],"dok":1,"skill":"graphic organizers"},{"q":"What is *eidiya*?","answer":"a gift of money that adults give children at Eid","explanation":"Glossary.","explanation_ar":"العيدية.","options":["a kind of sweet","a lantern","a prayer","a gift of money that adults give children at Eid"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which text has HEADINGS?","answer":"Eid Around the World","explanation":"Text features.","explanation_ar":"المقال.","options":["The Eid Lantern","both texts","neither text","Eid Around the World"],"dok":1,"skill":"text features"},{"q":"*Girls buy colorful **bangles**.* *Bangles* are ___.","answer":"round bracelets","explanation":"Bangle.","explanation_ar":"أساور.","options":["round bracelets","cookies","lanterns","rice baskets"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which sentence about the two Eid texts is correct?","answer":"Both texts are about Eid.","explanation":"Both → are.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Both texts are about Eid.","Both texts is about Eid.","Both text are about Eid.","Both texts being about Eid."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence contrasting the texts is correct?","answer":"Although the story has one family, the article has five countries.","explanation":"No but with although.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Although the story has one family, but the article has five countries.","Although, the story has one family the article has five countries.","Although the story has one family, the article has five countries.","The story although has one family."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What is the main PURPOSE of *Eid Around the World*?","answer":"to inform readers about Eid traditions in different countries","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"الإعلام.","options":["to tell a funny story","to persuade readers to travel","to teach how to make a lantern","to inform readers about Eid traditions in different countries"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose"},{"q":"Which idea belongs in the MIDDLE of the Venn diagram?","answer":"Eid is about spending time with the people we love.","explanation":"Both texts.","explanation_ar":"في الوسط.","options":["Noura's lantern breaks.","Ketupat is cooked in palm leaves.","Eid is about spending time with the people we love.","Mrs. Amal lives alone."],"dok":2,"skill":"compare texts"},{"q":"How does the story help you understand the article better?","answer":"It shows how sharing at Eid feels for real people.","explanation":"Synthesis.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر الشعور.","options":["It gives facts about Turkey.","It shows how sharing at Eid feels for real people.","It has more headings.","It lists five countries."],"dok":3,"skill":"synthesis"},{"q":"Which new heading would fit BEST in *Eid Around the World*?","answer":"Egypt – Families bake kahk, butter cookies covered in sugar.","explanation":"Text structure.","explanation_ar":"عنوان مناسب للمقال.","options":["Noura's Idea – She makes a jar lantern.","Egypt – Families bake kahk, butter cookies covered in sugar.","My Favorite Toy","How to Fix a Chair"],"dok":3,"skill":"text structure"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'eidiya': an adult giving money in an envelope to a child"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'village': a small village with green fields"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'woven': small baskets of woven palm leaves"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'forehead': a child touching an elder's hand to his forehead"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'henna': henna patterns on a hand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'bangle': colorful glass bangles"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'although': two different lanterns with one heart between them"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-01","prompt":"Eid traditions in five countries on a world map."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-02","prompt":"A story book and an information book compared."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS3-03","prompt":"A Venn diagram shaped like two lanterns."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V01","script":"eidiya. Grandpa gave us eidiya after the prayer."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V02","script":"village. They travel back to their home village."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V03","script":"woven. Ketupat is cooked in woven palm leaves."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V04","script":"forehead. He touched the hand to his forehead."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V05","script":"henna. Girls decorate their hands with henna."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V06","script":"bangle. She bought colorful bangles for Eid."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-V07","script":"although. Although the traditions are different, the meaning is the same."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-T1","script":"More than a billion Muslims celebrate Eid al-Fitr at the end of Ramadan. Everyone says \"Eid Mubarak,\" but each country has its own unique traditions. **Saudi Arabia** – After the Eid prayer, families visit their relatives, drink Arabic coffee and eat ma'amoul. Children receive eidiya, gifts of money from adults. **Indonesia** – In Indonesia, Eid is called Lebaran. Millions of people travel back to their home villages to celebrate with their families. A favorite food is ketupat, rice cooked inside little baskets of woven palm leaves. **Turkey** – Children kiss the hands of older relatives and touch them to their foreheads to show respect. In return, they get sweets or a little money, so some people call Eid the \"Sugar Festival.\" **Pakistan** – On Chand Raat, the night before Eid, girls and women decorate their hands with henna and shop for colorful bangles. **The United Kingdom** – Thousands of Muslims gather in big parks to pray together in the open air. Although the traditions are different, Eid everywhere is about the same things: thanking Allah, sharing food and spending time with the people we love."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS3-VID","script":"Eid Mubarak from around the world! Let's visit five countries in three minutes! More than a billion Muslims celebrate Eid al-Fitr… Story or article? The story has characters and feelings; the article has facts and headings. Venn diagram: only the story, only the article – and both in the middle! Different traditions, one message: share and spend time with the people you love."}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Describing with the five senses","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["describe a place or an event with details from all five senses (sight, hearing, smell, taste, touch)","use exact adjectives, strong verbs and similes (like / as … as)","turn 'telling' sentences into 'showing' sentences"],"objectives_ar":["وصف مكان أو حدث بتفاصيل من الحواس الخمس.","استخدام صفات دقيقة وأفعال قوية وتشبيهات.","تحويل جمل «الإخبار» إلى جمل «الإظهار»."],"success_criteria":["I can write a detail for each of the five senses.","I can use exact adjectives, strong verbs and a simile.","I can show a feeling instead of just telling it."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع كتابة تفصيل لكل حاسة من الحواس الخمس.","أستطيع استخدام صفات دقيقة وأفعال قوية وتشبيه.","أستطيع إظهار الشعور بدل الإخبار عنه فقط."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"senses","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɛnsɪz/","definition":"sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch","arabic":"الحواس","example":"Use all five senses in your description.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"Ardah","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɑrdə/","definition":"a traditional Saudi dance with drums, poetry and swords","arabic":"العرضة","example":"The men performed the Ardah on Founding Day.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"drift","pos":"verb","ipa":"/drɪft/","definition":"to move slowly through the air or on water","arabic":"ينجرف / يتسلل (الرائحة)","example":"The smell of coffee drifted through the park.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"smoky","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈsmoʊki/","definition":"tasting or smelling of smoke","arabic":"مدخّن / بطعم الدخان","example":"The bread tasted a little smoky.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"syrup","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪrəp/","definition":"a sweet, thick liquid","arabic":"دبس / شراب كثيف","example":"I dipped the bread in date syrup.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"heritage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɛrɪtɪdʒ/","definition":"the history, traditions and art that a country keeps from the past","arabic":"التراث","example":"We visited the heritage tent.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"simile","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪməli/","definition":"a comparison that uses like or as","arabic":"تشبيه","example":"'Rough like the coat of a sheep' is a simile.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A good description lets the reader **see, hear, smell, taste and touch** the place or event. Use details from the **five senses**: **sight** (colors, shapes, movement), **hearing** (sounds), **smell**, **taste** and **touch** (how things feel).\n\nChoose **exact adjectives** (*crunchy, smoky, rough, glittering*) and **strong verbs** (*drifted, boomed, sparkled* instead of *was, went*). Add a **simile** with **like** or **as … as**: *as bright as the moon*, *rough like the coat of a sheep*.\n\n**Show, don't tell.** Instead of *I was happy*, show it: *I couldn't stop smiling*. Put your details in a clear **order** (through the day or around the place) and end with a **feeling**.","examples":["Sight: ***Green flags waved from every lamp post.***","Hearing: ***The drums boomed like thunder.*** (simile)","Smell: ***The smell of cardamom coffee drifted from the stalls.***","Taste: ***The ma'amoul was crumbly and sweet, with soft dates inside.***","Touch: ***My new Eid clothes felt smooth and cool.***","Simile with as … as: ***The lanterns were as bright as stars.***","Telling → showing: *I was excited.* → ***I jumped out of bed before the alarm rang.***","Weak verb → strong verb: *The fireworks went up.* → ***The fireworks shot into the sky and burst into gold sparks.***","Closing feeling: ***I will never forget that magical day!***"],"common_errors":["✗ It was nice and good. → ✓ It was colorful, noisy and full of laughter. (Use **exact** adjectives.)","✗ Only using sight. → ✓ Add sounds, smells, tastes and touch. (Use all five senses.)","✗ The bread tasted good. → ✓ The bread tasted warm and a little smoky. (Say **how** it tasted.)","✗ The drums were like loud. → ✓ The drums were as loud as thunder. (**as + adjective + as**.)","✗ I was very very happy. → ✓ I couldn't stop smiling. (Show, don't tell.)","✗ The smell of coffee were strong. → ✓ The smell of coffee was strong. (The smell (one) → **was**.)","✗ The rug felt roughly. → ✓ The rug felt rough. (feel + **adjective**.)"],"arabic":"الوصف الجيد يجعل القارئ يرى ويسمع ويشم ويتذوق ويلمس المكان أو الحدث.\n\nاختر صفات دقيقة (crunchy, smoky, rough) وأفعالًا قوية (drifted, boomed) وأضف تشبيهًا بـ like أو as … as.\n\nأظهِر ولا تُخبر: بدل «كنت سعيدًا» قل «لم أستطع التوقف عن الابتسام». رتّب التفاصيل واختم بشعور.\n\nبعد feel وtaste وsmell نستخدم صفة لا ظرفًا: felt rough وليس felt roughly."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Close your eyes… Can you hear the drums? Can you smell the coffee? Welcome to Founding Day!","on_screen":"Describing with the Five Senses 👁️👂👃👅✋","visual":"Sami closes his eyes in a busy park; sense icons float around him."},{"time":"00:20–01:20","narration":"On Founding Day, our neighborhood park looked like a page from a history book…","on_screen":"(Sami's paragraph)","visual":"Each sense detail appears with its icon: flags, drums, coffee, flatbread, a woven rug, fireworks."},{"time":"01:20–02:00","narration":"Exact adjectives and strong verbs: the drums BOOMED, the smell DRIFTED!","on_screen":"nice → colorful · went → shot","visual":"Weak words fade and strong words pop in."},{"time":"02:00–02:40","narration":"Similes: as bright as stars… rough like the coat of a sheep!","on_screen":"like · as … as","visual":"Pictures connect: a rug and a sheep; lanterns and stars."},{"time":"02:40–03:00","narration":"Show, don't tell: I couldn't stop smiling!","on_screen":"Show, don't tell 😊","visual":"Sami grins while fireworks burst in the sky."}],"captions_ar":["أغمض عينيك… هل تسمع الطبول؟ هل تشم رائحة القهوة؟ مرحبًا بك في يوم التأسيس!","في يوم التأسيس بدت حديقة حينا كصفحة من كتاب تاريخ…","صفات دقيقة وأفعال قوية: دوّت الطبول، وانسابت الرائحة!","تشبيهات: مضيئة كالنجوم… خشنة كصوف الخروف!","أظهِر ولا تُخبر: لم أستطع التوقف عن الابتسام!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm female teacher, speed 0.90. Sami reads his paragraph."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Founding Day at the Park (by Sami)","body":"On Founding Day, our neighborhood park looked like a page from a history book. Everywhere I looked, I saw green flags waving and families in traditional clothes and colorful scarves. I heard the deep boom of drums as a group of men performed the Ardah, and the crowd clapped along to the beat. The smell of fresh bread and cardamom coffee drifted from the food stalls. Grandpa bought me a warm piece of flatbread. It tasted soft and a little smoky, and I dipped it in sweet date syrup. In the heritage tent, I ran my hand over an old woven rug. It felt rough and thick, like the coat of a sheep. As the sun went down, fireworks filled the sky with gold and green sparks. I will never forget that magical day!","word_count":134,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L04-U6-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"450L–600L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Topic sentence with a simile:** *the park looked **like** a page from a history book* – a picture in the reader's mind.","**Sight:** *green flags waving, colorful scarves, gold and green sparks*.","**Hearing:** *the deep **boom** of drums, the crowd **clapped** along to the beat*.","**Smell:** *fresh bread and cardamom coffee **drifted** from the food stalls* – a strong verb.","**Taste:** *soft and a little smoky … sweet date syrup*.","**Touch:** *rough and thick, **like** the coat of a sheep* – another simile.","**Order + closing:** the paragraph moves through the day (*As the sun went down*) and ends with a feeling: *I will never forget that magical day!*"]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Sami's paragraph. Choose the best answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ فقرة سامي واختر أفضل إجابة.","items":[{"q":"What did the park look like on Founding Day, according to Sami?","answer":"a page from a history book","explanation":"Simile.","explanation_ar":"صفحة من كتاب تاريخ.","options":["a football field","a busy airport","a page from a history book","a quiet desert"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Sami HEAR in the park?","answer":"the deep boom of drums","explanation":"Hearing.","explanation_ar":"صوت الطبول.","options":["the deep boom of drums","the smell of bread","the taste of syrup","the feel of a rug"],"dok":1,"skill":"senses","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Sami dip his flatbread in?","answer":"sweet date syrup","explanation":"Taste.","explanation_ar":"دبس التمر.","options":["honey","coffee","milk","sweet date syrup"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How did the old woven rug feel?","answer":"rough and thick","explanation":"Touch.","explanation_ar":"خشن وسميك.","options":["smooth and cold","wet and soft","hot and sticky","rough and thick"],"dok":1,"skill":"senses","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which sense does *The smell of fresh bread and cardamom coffee drifted from the food stalls* use?","answer":"smell","explanation":"Smell.","explanation_ar":"الشم.","options":["sight","smell","touch","taste"],"dok":1,"skill":"senses","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How does Sami end his paragraph?","answer":"with a feeling: I will never forget that magical day!","explanation":"Closing.","explanation_ar":"بشعور.","options":["with a feeling: I will never forget that magical day!","with a question","with a list of foods","with the date of Founding Day"],"dok":2,"skill":"closings","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Senses, similes and strong words. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الحواس والتشبيهات والكلمات القوية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which detail about Eid morning uses the sense of TASTE?","answer":"The ma'amoul was crumbly and sweet.","explanation":"Taste.","explanation_ar":"التذوق.","options":["The lanterns were bright.","The doorbell rang again and again.","The ma'amoul was crumbly and sweet.","My new clothes felt smooth."],"dok":1,"skill":"senses","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the Founding Day drums uses a SIMILE?","answer":"The drums boomed like thunder.","explanation":"like.","explanation_ar":"تشبيه.","options":["The drums were loud.","The drums were big.","I saw the drums.","The drums boomed like thunder."],"dok":1,"skill":"similes","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the fireworks uses the STRONGEST verbs?","answer":"The fireworks shot into the sky and burst into gold sparks.","explanation":"Strong verbs.","explanation_ar":"أفعال قوية.","options":["The fireworks went up.","The fireworks shot into the sky and burst into gold sparks.","The fireworks were there.","There were fireworks."],"dok":2,"skill":"word choice","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence SHOWS that Layla was excited on Eid morning?","answer":"Layla jumped out of bed before the alarm rang.","explanation":"Show, don't tell.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر الحماس.","options":["Layla was excited.","Layla felt very excited.","Layla jumped out of bed before the alarm rang.","Layla was happy and excited."],"dok":2,"skill":"show, don't tell","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which simile about the lanterns is written correctly?","answer":"The lanterns were as bright as stars.","explanation":"as … as.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The lanterns were as bright as stars.","The lanterns were as bright like stars.","The lanterns were bright as like stars.","The lanterns were like as bright stars."],"dok":2,"skill":"similes","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the new Eid dress is correct?","answer":"My new Eid dress felt soft.","explanation":"feel + adjective.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["My new Eid dress felt softly.","My new Eid dress felt soft.","My new Eid dress feeled soft.","My new Eid dress feel softly."],"dok":3,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the Eid description with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل وصف العيد من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"The house ________ with the smell of incense and coffee.","answer":"filled","explanation":"Strong verb.","explanation_ar":"امتلأ.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The children's laughter was as ________ as music.","answer":"cheerful","explanation":"Exact adjective.","explanation_ar":"مبهجة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The warm ma'amoul was ________ on the outside and soft inside.","answer":"crumbly","explanation":"Taste/touch.","explanation_ar":"هشّ.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Lanterns ________ on every balcony like little moons.","answer":"glowed","explanation":"Strong verb + simile.","explanation_ar":"توهّجت.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The silk ribbon on my gift felt ________ and cool.","answer":"smooth","explanation":"Touch.","explanation_ar":"ناعم.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Fireworks ________ above the city at night.","answer":"sparkled","explanation":"Strong verb.","explanation_ar":"تلألأت.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write sensory sentences.","instructions_ar":"اكتب جملًا حسية.","items":[{"q":"Change this telling sentence into a showing sentence: *I was happy on Eid.*","answer":"I couldn't stop smiling when Grandpa gave me my eidiya.","explanation":"Show, don't tell.","explanation_ar":"أظهِر.","dok":2,"skill":"show, don't tell","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a simile about the Eid lanterns or the Founding Day flags.","answer":"The green flags danced in the wind like waves in the sea.","explanation":"Simile.","explanation_ar":"تشبيه.","dok":2,"skill":"similes","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence for HEARING and one for SMELL at an Eid gathering.","answer":"I heard my cousins laughing in the garden. The smell of grilled meat drifted from the kitchen.","explanation":"Two senses.","explanation_ar":"حاستان.","dok":2,"skill":"senses","stage":"production"},{"q":"Improve this sentence with exact adjectives and a strong verb: *The food was good and the coffee was there.*","answer":"The rice was spicy and steaming, and the cardamom coffee poured from Grandpa's shiny pot.","explanation":"Word choice.","explanation_ar":"اختيار الكلمات.","dok":3,"skill":"word choice","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Five Senses Lantern","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Sense lantern: a celebration picture appears; the child drags sense words to the right panel of the lantern (sight, hearing, smell, taste, touch). Each correct panel lights up; a simile bonus makes the lantern sparkle.","Each team gets a celebration picture card and a lantern template.","Team members write one detail on each panel: sight, hearing, smell, taste, touch.","They add one simile to the top of the lantern.","Teams read their lanterns aloud; others check the senses.","Each correct panel = 1 point; a good simile = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What are the five senses?","answer":"sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch","explanation":"Senses.","explanation_ar":"البصر والسمع والشم والتذوق واللمس.","options":["red, blue, green, yellow and black","happy, sad, angry, tired and excited","sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch","first, next, then, finally and last"],"dok":1,"skill":"senses"},{"q":"A comparison that uses *like* or *as* is a ___.","answer":"simile","explanation":"Simile.","explanation_ar":"تشبيه.","options":["simile","symbol","summary","subject"],"dok":1,"skill":"similes"},{"q":"*The smell of coffee **drifted** through the house.* *Drifted* means ___.","answer":"moved slowly through the air","explanation":"Drift.","explanation_ar":"انساب ببطء.","options":["fell down quickly","moved slowly through the air","stopped","got cold"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which detail about Founding Day uses the sense of HEARING?","answer":"The crowd clapped along to the drums.","explanation":"Hearing.","explanation_ar":"السمع.","options":["The flags were green.","The bread was warm.","The crowd clapped along to the drums.","The rug was rough."],"dok":1,"skill":"senses"},{"q":"Which sentence about Grandpa's coffee is correct?","answer":"The smell of Grandpa's coffee was strong.","explanation":"The smell → was.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The smell of Grandpa's coffee were strong.","The smell of Grandpa's coffee was strong.","The smell of Grandpa's coffee are strong.","The smell of Grandpa's coffee strongly."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence uses an EXACT adjective instead of *nice*?","answer":"The cake was soft and sticky with honey.","explanation":"Exact adjectives.","explanation_ar":"صفة دقيقة.","options":["The cake was nice.","The cake was good.","The cake was OK.","The cake was soft and sticky with honey."],"dok":2,"skill":"word choice"},{"q":"Which sentence SHOWS that the guests enjoyed the feast?","answer":"The guests asked for a second plate and licked their fingers.","explanation":"Show, don't tell.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر الاستمتاع.","options":["The guests enjoyed the feast.","The guests were happy.","The feast was enjoyed.","The guests asked for a second plate and licked their fingers."],"dok":2,"skill":"show, don't tell"},{"q":"Which simile about the Eid fireworks is BEST?","answer":"The fireworks opened like giant golden flowers.","explanation":"Fresh simile.","explanation_ar":"أفضل تشبيه.","options":["The fireworks were like fireworks.","The fireworks opened like giant golden flowers.","The fireworks were as fireworks as fireworks.","The fireworks were nice like things."],"dok":3,"skill":"similes"},{"q":"Why do writers use details from all five senses?","answer":"to help readers feel as if they are really there","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"ليشعر القارئ أنه هناك.","options":["to make the paragraph shorter","to list facts in order","to help readers feel as if they are really there","to ask the reader questions"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"Which order is BEST for Sami's Founding Day paragraph?","answer":"from morning to sunset, ending with a feeling","explanation":"Order.","explanation_ar":"من الصباح حتى الغروب.","options":["from morning to sunset, ending with a feeling","from sunset back to morning","all the tastes first, then nothing else","in ABC order"],"dok":3,"skill":"organization"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'senses': icons of an eye, ear, nose, tongue and hand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'Ardah': men performing the Ardah with drums"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'drift': steam drifting from a coffee pot"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'smoky': bread on a fire"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'syrup': a bowl of dark date syrup"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'heritage': old pots, rugs and a traditional door"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'simile': two pictures joined by the word like"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-01","prompt":"A Founding Day celebration in a park."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-02","prompt":"The five senses with celebration examples."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS4-03","prompt":"Simile picture pairs."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V01","script":"senses. Use all five senses in your description."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V02","script":"Ardah. The men performed the Ardah on Founding Day."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V03","script":"drift. The smell of coffee drifted through the park."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V04","script":"smoky. The bread tasted a little smoky."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V05","script":"syrup. I dipped the bread in date syrup."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V06","script":"heritage. We visited the heritage tent."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-V07","script":"simile. 'Rough like the coat of a sheep' is a simile."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-T1","script":"On Founding Day, our neighborhood park looked like a page from a history book. Everywhere I looked, I saw green flags waving and families in traditional clothes and colorful scarves. I heard the deep boom of drums as a group of men performed the Ardah, and the crowd clapped along to the beat. The smell of fresh bread and cardamom coffee drifted from the food stalls. Grandpa bought me a warm piece of flatbread. It tasted soft and a little smoky, and I dipped it in sweet date syrup. In the heritage tent, I ran my hand over an old woven rug. It felt rough and thick, like the coat of a sheep. As the sun went down, fireworks filled the sky with gold and green sparks. I will never forget that magical day!"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS4-VID","script":"Close your eyes… Can you hear the drums? Can you smell the coffee? Welcome to Founding Day! On Founding Day, our neighborhood park looked like a page from a history book… Exact adjectives and strong verbs: the drums BOOMED, the smell DRIFTED! Similes: as bright as stars… rough like the coat of a sheep! Show, don't tell: I couldn't stop smiling!"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: Our Eid Morning","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a descriptive paragraph about an Eid morning with the Five Senses Map","write 8–10 sentences in time order with details from all five senses, exact words and at least one simile","revise, edit and publish the paragraph in the class book 'Our Celebrations'"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لفقرة وصفية عن صباح العيد بخريطة الحواس الخمس.","كتابة 8–10 جمل بالترتيب الزمني بتفاصيل من الحواس الخمس وكلمات دقيقة وتشبيه واحد على الأقل.","تحسين الفقرة وتدقيقها ونشرها في كتاب الصف «احتفالاتنا»."],"success_criteria":["My topic sentence tells the time, the place and a feeling.","I used details from all five senses, strong verbs and exact adjectives.","I used a simile and showed a feeling, and my closing sentence ends with a feeling."],"success_criteria_ar":["جملتي الرئيسية تذكر الزمان والمكان وشعورًا.","استخدمت تفاصيل من الحواس الخمس وأفعالًا قوية وصفات دقيقة.","استخدمت تشبيهًا وأظهرت شعورًا، واختتمت بشعور."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"takbeer","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tækˈbir/","definition":"saying 'Allahu Akbar' (God is the Greatest), heard before the Eid prayer","arabic":"التكبير","example":"We heard the takbeer from the mosque.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"incense","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪnsɛns/","definition":"wood or powder that smells nice when you burn it (bukhoor)","arabic":"بخور","example":"The house smelled of incense.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"crisp","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/krɪsp/","definition":"fresh, clean and a little stiff","arabic":"جديد ومشدود (للقماش) / مقرمش","example":"My new thobe felt crisp and cool.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"buttery","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈbʌtəri/","definition":"tasting of or full of butter","arabic":"زبدي","example":"The ma'amoul was warm and buttery.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"sticky","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈstɪki/","definition":"soft and able to stick to things","arabic":"لزج","example":"My fingers were sticky from the dates.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"crumble","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈkrʌmbəl/","definition":"to break into very small pieces","arabic":"يتفتّت","example":"The ma'amoul crumbled in my mouth.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"descriptive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/dɪˈskrɪptɪv/","definition":"giving a clear picture of something with details","arabic":"وصفي","example":"Write a descriptive paragraph about Eid.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **descriptive paragraph** paints a picture with words. The reader should feel as if he or she is **there** with you on Eid morning.\n\nPlan with the **Five Senses Map**: a **topic sentence** (when, where and a feeling), details for **sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch**, and a **closing** with a feeling. Put the details in **time order**: waking up → the prayer → relatives → food → the end of the morning.\n\nMake every sentence strong: **exact adjectives** (*crisp, buttery, golden*), **strong verbs** (*floated, crumbled, filled*), at least one **simile** (*like a song*, *as loud as birds*) and **show, don't tell** (*my cheeks hurt from smiling*).","examples":["Topic sentence: ***Eid morning in our house is the most magical time of the year.***","Hearing + simile: ***When I wake up, I hear the takbeer from the mosque floating through my window like a song.***","Smell: ***The whole house smells of incense and fresh coffee.***","Touch: ***I put on my new white thobe, which feels crisp and cool on my skin.***","Sight + hearing: ***After the Eid prayer, our relatives fill the living room, and everyone hugs and says, \"Eid Mubarak!\"***","Sight: ***The table is covered with plates of golden ma'amoul, sticky dates and colorful sweets.***","Taste: ***I bite into a warm ma'amoul, and it crumbles in my mouth, sweet and buttery.***","Simile: ***My little cousins run around the room, laughing as loudly as birds in the morning.***","Closing (show the feeling): ***By noon, my cheeks hurt from smiling, and I wish Eid could last forever.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Writing a list of things you did. → ✓ Describe how things looked, sounded, smelled, tasted and felt. (Describe, don't list.)","✗ It was a nice day and the food was good. → ✓ The table was covered with golden ma'amoul and sticky dates. (Exact words.)","✗ Mixing tenses: I wake up and I put on … then I ate. → ✓ Keep one tense: I wake up … I put on … I eat. (Stay in one tense.)","✗ Missing a sense. → ✓ Check your map: one detail for each sense. (All five senses.)","✗ as loud like birds → ✓ as loudly as birds / as loud as birds (**as … as**.)","✗ Ending with 'The end.' → ✓ End with a feeling: I wish Eid could last forever. (Feeling closing.)","✗ Copying the sense cards. → ✓ Turn notes into your own full sentences. (Own words.)"],"arabic":"الفقرة الوصفية ترسم صورة بالكلمات، فيشعر القارئ أنه معك صباح العيد.\n\nخطّط بخريطة الحواس: جملة رئيسية (الزمان والمكان وشعور)، وتفاصيل للبصر والسمع والشم والتذوق واللمس، وخاتمة بشعور، ورتّب التفاصيل زمنيًا.\n\nقوِّ كل جملة: صفات دقيقة وأفعال قوية وتشبيه واحد على الأقل، وأظهِر الشعور بدل الإخبار عنه.\n\nحافظ على زمن واحد في الفقرة كلها، ولا تنسخ بطاقات الحواس كما هي."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Close your eyes. It's Eid morning… What do you see, hear, smell, taste and feel?","on_screen":"Our Eid Morning 🌙✨","visual":"A bedroom at sunrise; a new thobe hangs on the door."},{"time":"00:20–01:00","narration":"Read the sense cards: see, hear, smell, taste, touch!","on_screen":"Five Senses Map","visual":"Five cards fly into the five arms of a star-shaped map."},{"time":"01:00–01:50","narration":"Eid morning in our house is the most magical time of the year…","on_screen":"(model paragraph)","visual":"The paragraph is acted out: takbeer, incense, the new thobe, relatives hugging, ma'amoul, laughing cousins."},{"time":"01:50–02:35","narration":"Exact words, strong verbs, a simile – and show your feelings!","on_screen":"crisp · crumbled · like a song","visual":"Key words glow in the paragraph."},{"time":"02:35–03:00","narration":"Revise, edit and publish your paragraph in our class book: Our Celebrations!","on_screen":"Our Celebrations 📚","visual":"Pages with drawings fly into a class book."}],"captions_ar":["أغمض عينيك. إنه صباح العيد… ماذا ترى وتسمع وتشم وتتذوق وتلمس؟","اقرأ بطاقات الحواس: البصر والسمع والشم والتذوق واللمس!","صباح العيد في بيتنا أجمل أوقات السنة…","كلمات دقيقة وأفعال قوية وتشبيه، وأظهِر مشاعرك!","حسّن ودقّق وانشر فقرتك في كتاب صفنا: احتفالاتنا!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm female voice, speed 0.90. Omar reads the model paragraph."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Eid Morning Sense Cards","body":"**See** · new clothes · lanterns and paper stars · relatives hugging · plates of ma'amoul and dates · shiny eidiya envelopes\n\n**Hear** · the takbeer from the mosque · \"Eid Mubarak!\" · the doorbell ringing again and again · children laughing\n\n**Smell** · incense (bukhoor) · fresh Arabic coffee with cardamom · perfume · sweets baking in the oven\n\n**Taste** · warm, buttery ma'amoul · sticky, sweet dates · bitter coffee · cold juice\n\n**Touch** · crisp new clothes · a warm hug from Grandma · the paper of an eidiya envelope · soft cushions","word_count":75,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L04-U6-LS5-1","genre":"sense cards","role":"model","difficulty":"450L–600L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **at least one detail for each sense**. You don't need to use every idea.","Change the notes into **full sentences** with **strong verbs** and **exact adjectives**.","Add **one or two similes** and **show** your feelings instead of only telling them."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the sense cards and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ البطاقات والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Which card lists *the takbeer from the mosque*?","answer":"Hear","explanation":"Sense cards.","explanation_ar":"السمع.","options":["See","Taste","Touch","Hear"],"dok":1,"skill":"senses","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does the house smell of in the model?","answer":"incense and fresh coffee","explanation":"Model.","explanation_ar":"البخور والقهوة.","options":["rain and grass","incense and fresh coffee","the sea","smoke from a fire"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How does the new thobe feel?","answer":"crisp and cool","explanation":"Touch.","explanation_ar":"جديد ومشدود وبارد.","options":["crisp and cool","rough and hot","wet and heavy","soft and sticky"],"dok":1,"skill":"senses","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is on the Eid table in the model?","answer":"golden ma'amoul, sticky dates and colorful sweets","explanation":"Sight.","explanation_ar":"معمول وتمر وحلويات.","options":["rice and chicken only","fruit and vegetables","bread and cheese","golden ma'amoul, sticky dates and colorful sweets"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does the writer compare the takbeer to?","answer":"a song","explanation":"Simile.","explanation_ar":"أغنية.","options":["a song","thunder","birds","a bell"],"dok":2,"skill":"similes","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does *my cheeks hurt from smiling* show?","answer":"The writer was very happy.","explanation":"Show, don't tell.","explanation_ar":"كان سعيدًا جدًا.","options":["The writer was hurt.","The writer was cold.","The writer was very happy.","The writer was tired of Eid."],"dok":2,"skill":"show, don't tell","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan the description. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطّط للوصف: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST topic sentence for *Our Eid Morning*?","answer":"Eid morning in our house is the most magical time of the year.","explanation":"When, where, feeling.","explanation_ar":"الأفضل.","options":["Eid morning in our house is the most magical time of the year.","I ate ma'amoul.","Eid is a word.","My cousin is seven."],"dok":1,"skill":"topic sentences","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which detail about the Eid guests uses the sense of TOUCH?","answer":"Grandma gave me a warm, tight hug.","explanation":"Touch.","explanation_ar":"اللمس.","options":["The guests wore new clothes.","The guests said Eid Mubarak.","The guests smelled of perfume.","Grandma gave me a warm, tight hug."],"dok":1,"skill":"senses","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which order of details is BEST for an Eid morning paragraph?","answer":"waking up → the prayer → relatives arrive → food","explanation":"Time order.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب الزمني.","options":["food → waking up → the prayer → relatives arrive","relatives arrive → waking up → food → the prayer","the prayer → food → waking up → relatives arrive","waking up → the prayer → relatives arrive → food"],"dok":2,"skill":"organization","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the Eid sweets uses EXACT adjectives?","answer":"The ma'amoul was warm, crumbly and buttery.","explanation":"Exact words.","explanation_ar":"صفات دقيقة.","options":["The ma'amoul was warm, crumbly and buttery.","The ma'amoul was nice.","The ma'amoul was good and nice.","The ma'amoul was a thing."],"dok":2,"skill":"word choice","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence keeps the SAME tense as *When I wake up, I hear the takbeer*?","answer":"I put on my new thobe.","explanation":"Present tense.","explanation_ar":"الزمن نفسه.","options":["I put on my new thobe yesterday and ate.","I will put on my new thobe last year.","I put on my new thobe.","I was putting on my new thobe tomorrow."],"dok":3,"skill":"verb tense","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which closing sentence for the Eid paragraph SHOWS a feeling?","answer":"By noon, my cheeks hurt from smiling.","explanation":"Show the feeling.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر الشعور.","options":["The end.","By noon, my cheeks hurt from smiling.","That is my Eid.","Eid was on Tuesday."],"dok":2,"skill":"closings","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your paragraph with the Five Senses Map.","instructions_ar":"خطط لفقرتك بخريطة الحواس الخمس.","items":[{"q":"Topic sentence – when, where and a feeling.","answer":"Eid morning at my grandparents' house is full of joy.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"الجملة الرئيسية.","dok":1,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Sight and hearing – write notes for each.","answer":"Sight: green and gold balloons, my cousins in new clothes. Hearing: the takbeer, the doorbell ringing again and again.","explanation":"Two senses.","explanation_ar":"حاستان.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Smell, taste and touch – write notes for each.","answer":"Smell: bukhoor and cardamom coffee. Taste: sticky dates. Touch: the smooth paper of my eidiya envelope.","explanation":"Three senses.","explanation_ar":"ثلاث حواس.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"A simile and a closing feeling – write notes.","answer":"Simile: the house was as busy as a beehive. Closing: I wished the morning would never end.","explanation":"Simile + closing.","explanation_ar":"تشبيه + خاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and publish your paragraph.","instructions_ar":"اكتب فقرتك وحسّنها ودققها وانشرها.","items":[{"q":"Write your topic sentence and two sense sentences.","answer":"Eid morning at my grandparents' house is full of joy. When I arrive, I hear the doorbell ringing again and again. The air smells of bukhoor and cardamom coffee.","explanation":"Opening.","explanation_ar":"البداية.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write three more sense sentences with exact words and a simile.","answer":"My cousins run in their new clothes, and the house is as busy as a beehive. Grandpa gives me a smooth paper envelope with my eidiya. I eat a sticky date that tastes as sweet as honey.","explanation":"Middle.","explanation_ar":"الوسط.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write your closing sentence with a feeling.","answer":"When everyone sits down together, I feel so warm inside that I wish the morning would never end.","explanation":"Closing.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one Eid sentence you made more descriptive after the Two-Partner Edit (before → after).","answer":"Before: The food was good. After: The table was covered with warm ma'amoul and sticky dates.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Five Senses Map","instructions":"Organizer: Use the sense cards and your own memories. Write short notes for each box.","items":[{"q":"Topic sentence – when, where, feeling","answer":"Eid morning in our house – the most magical time of the year"},{"q":"Sight","answer":"relatives hugging; golden ma'amoul, sticky dates, colorful sweets; shiny eidiya envelopes"},{"q":"Hearing","answer":"takbeer floating through my window like a song; 'Eid Mubarak!'; cousins laughing as loudly as birds"},{"q":"Smell","answer":"incense and fresh coffee"},{"q":"Taste","answer":"warm ma'amoul crumbles – sweet and buttery"},{"q":"Touch","answer":"new white thobe – crisp and cool"},{"q":"Closing – a feeling","answer":"cheeks hurt from smiling; wish Eid could last forever"}]},{"id":"RW-L04-U6-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Write a descriptive paragraph (8–10 sentences) called 'Our Eid Morning'. Use details from all five senses in time order, exact adjectives, strong verbs, at least one simile and a closing sentence with a feeling. Write a descriptive paragraph (8–10 sentences) called 'Our Eid Morning'. Use details from all five senses in time order, exact adjectives, strong verbs, at least one simile and a closing sentence with a feeling.","instructions_ar":"اكتب فقرة وصفية (8–10 جمل) بعنوان «صباح عيدنا» بتفاصيل من الحواس الخمس بالترتيب الزمني، وصفات دقيقة وأفعال قوية وتشبيه واحد على الأقل، وجملة ختامية بشعور.","prompt":"Write a descriptive paragraph (8–10 sentences) called 'Our Eid Morning'. Use details from all five senses in time order, exact adjectives, strong verbs, at least one simile and a closing sentence with a feeling.","checklist":["My topic sentence tells when, where and a feeling.","I used details from all five senses.","My details are in time order.","I used exact adjectives (not nice or good).","I used strong verbs.","I used at least one simile (like / as … as).","I kept the same tense in the whole paragraph.","My closing sentence shows a feeling, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":90,"max_words":180,"organizer":"Five Senses Map (topic sentence – sight – hearing – smell – taste – touch – closing)","items":[{"q":"Write a descriptive paragraph (8–10 sentences) called 'Our Eid Morning'. Use details from all five senses in time order, exact adjectives, strong verbs, at least one simile and a closing sentence with a feeling.","answer":"Eid morning in our house is the most magical time of the year. When I wake up, I hear the takbeer from the mosque floating through my window like a song. The whole house smells of incense and fresh coffee. I put on my new white thobe, which feels crisp and cool on my skin. After the Eid prayer, our relatives fill the living room, and everyone hugs and says, \"Eid Mubarak!\" The table is covered with plates of golden ma'amoul, sticky dates and colorful sweets. I bite into a warm ma'amoul, and it crumbles in my mouth, sweet and buttery. My little cousins run around the room, laughing as loudly as birds in the morning. By noon, my cheeks hurt from smiling, and I wish Eid could last forever.","_w":{"checklist":["My topic sentence tells when, where and a feeling.","I used details from all five senses.","My details are in time order.","I used exact adjectives (not nice or good).","I used strong verbs.","I used at least one simile (like / as … as).","I kept the same tense in the whole paragraph.","My closing sentence shows a feeling, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"min":90,"max":180}}]}],"game":{"name":"Sense Detective Gallery","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Sense scanner: the child reads a description and taps each sense detail; the matching sense icon fills with color. Finding a simile earns a shining star.","Paragraphs are placed around the room like a gallery.","Students walk in pairs and read three paragraphs.","They add a colored sticky note for each sense they find.","They put a star next to the best simile.","The paragraph with all five colors and a star wins a 'Master Describer' badge."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What does a *descriptive* paragraph do?","answer":"paints a picture with words using details","explanation":"Descriptive.","explanation_ar":"يرسم صورة بالكلمات.","options":["paints a picture with words using details","gives numbered steps","tells only facts with dates","asks the reader questions"],"dok":1,"skill":"genre"},{"q":"*The house smelled of **incense**.* *Incense* is ___.","answer":"something that smells nice when you burn it","explanation":"Incense.","explanation_ar":"بخور.","options":["a kind of sweet","a new dress","a loud sound","something that smells nice when you burn it"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word describes the TASTE of ma'amoul?","answer":"buttery","explanation":"Taste.","explanation_ar":"زبدي.","options":["crisp","loud","buttery","golden"],"dok":1,"skill":"senses"},{"q":"Which detail about the Eid gathering uses the sense of SMELL?","answer":"the scent of perfume and fresh coffee","explanation":"Smell.","explanation_ar":"الشم.","options":["the doorbell ringing","a warm hug","golden lanterns","the scent of perfume and fresh coffee"],"dok":1,"skill":"senses"},{"q":"Which simile about the cousins is written correctly?","answer":"My cousins laughed as loudly as birds.","explanation":"as … as.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["My cousins laughed as loudly like birds.","My cousins laughed as loudly as birds.","My cousins laughed as loud than birds.","My cousins laughed like as loudly birds."],"dok":2,"skill":"similes"},{"q":"Which sentence keeps the past tense in a paragraph about LAST Eid?","answer":"We visited Grandma, and she gave us dates.","explanation":"One tense.","explanation_ar":"زمن واحد.","options":["We visited Grandma, and she gives us dates.","We visit Grandma, and she gave us dates.","We visited Grandma, and she gave us dates.","We will visit Grandma, and she gave us dates."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense"},{"q":"Which sentence about the ma'amoul uses a STRONG verb?","answer":"The ma'amoul crumbled in my mouth.","explanation":"Strong verb.","explanation_ar":"فعل قوي.","options":["The ma'amoul crumbled in my mouth.","The ma'amoul was in my mouth.","The ma'amoul went in my mouth.","The ma'amoul is there."],"dok":2,"skill":"word choice"},{"q":"Why should your details be in time order?","answer":"so the reader can follow the morning from start to end","explanation":"Organization.","explanation_ar":"ليتابع القارئ الصباح.","options":["so the paragraph is shorter","so the reader can follow the morning from start to end","so every sentence starts with I","so you don't need a closing"],"dok":2,"skill":"organization"},{"q":"Which sentence SHOWS that the family was together and happy?","answer":"Everyone squeezed onto the big sofa for a family photo, laughing.","explanation":"Show, don't tell.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر السعادة.","options":["The family was happy.","Everyone squeezed onto the big sofa for a family photo, laughing.","We were together.","It was a family day."],"dok":3,"skill":"show, don't tell"},{"q":"A partner says, *Your paragraph has no sense of touch.* What is the BEST sentence to add?","answer":"My new shoes felt stiff and shiny on my feet.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أضف تفصيلًا للّمس.","options":["I like Eid.","The sweets were nice.","My new shoes felt stiff and shiny on my feet.","We went home."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'takbeer': a mosque at sunrise"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'incense': smoke rising from an incense burner"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'crisp': a folded new white thobe"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'buttery': a plate of golden cookies"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'sticky': dates on a plate"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'crumble': a cookie breaking into crumbs"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'descriptive': a paragraph with sense icons"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-01","prompt":"A class book of Eid descriptions."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-02","prompt":"Five Eid sense cards."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L04-U6-LS5-03","prompt":"A five senses map organizer."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V01","script":"takbeer. We heard the takbeer from the mosque."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V02","script":"incense. The house smelled of incense."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V03","script":"crisp. My new thobe felt crisp and cool."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V04","script":"buttery. The ma'amoul was warm and buttery."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V05","script":"sticky. My fingers were sticky from the dates."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V06","script":"crumble. The ma'amoul crumbled in my mouth."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-V07","script":"descriptive. Write a descriptive paragraph about Eid."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-T1","script":"**See** · new clothes · lanterns and paper stars · relatives hugging · plates of ma'amoul and dates · shiny eidiya envelopes **Hear** · the takbeer from the mosque · \"Eid Mubarak!\" · the doorbell ringing again and again · children laughing **Smell** · incense (bukhoor) · fresh Arabic coffee with cardamom · perfume · sweets baking in the oven **Taste** · warm, buttery ma'amoul · sticky, sweet dates · bitter coffee · cold juice **Touch** · crisp new clothes · a warm hug from Grandma · the paper of an eidiya envelope · soft cushions"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L04-U6-LS5-VID","script":"Close your eyes. It's Eid morning… What do you see, hear, smell, taste and feel? Read the sense cards: see, hear, smell, taste, touch! Eid morning in our house is the most magical time of the year… Exact words, strong verbs, a simile – and show your feelings! Revise, edit and publish your paragraph in our class book: Our Celebrations!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"After the Eid prayer, the whole family enjoyed a big ___ of rice, meat and sweets.","answer":"feast","explanation":"Feast.","explanation_ar":"وليمة.","options":["lantern","feast","symbol","guest"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"My aunts, uncles and cousins are all my ___.","answer":"relatives","explanation":"Relatives.","explanation_ar":"أقاربي.","options":["neighbors","guests","traditions","relatives"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"We hung green flags to ___ our school for Founding Day.","answer":"decorate","explanation":"Decorate.","explanation_ar":"نزيّن.","options":["invite","gather","decorate","survive"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Noura asked Mrs. Amal to come to breakfast. She ___ her.","answer":"invited","explanation":"Invite.","explanation_ar":"دعتها.","options":["invited","decorated","celebrated","crumbled"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Serving coffee and dates to every visitor is an example of Saudi ___.","answer":"hospitality","explanation":"Hospitality.","explanation_ar":"كرم الضيافة.","options":["temperature","hospitality","environment","budget"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Grandpa tells the same Ramadan stories every year. It is a family **tradition**.* *Tradition* means ___.","answer":"something done the same way for many years","explanation":"Tradition.","explanation_ar":"تقليد.","options":["a new toy","a kind of food","something done the same way for many years","a long trip"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The box of sweets was **light**, so Dana carried it to the table.* What does *light* mean here?","answer":"not heavy","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"خفيف.","options":["not heavy","a lamp","bright","early"],"dok":2,"skill":"multiple meanings","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Everyone in our family was **present** at Grandpa's house for Eid.* What does *present* mean here?","answer":"here, not absent","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"حاضر.","options":["a gift","the time now","a show","here, not absent"],"dok":2,"skill":"multiple meanings","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which detail about the iftar boxes uses the sense of SMELL?","answer":"The warm samosas smelled of spices.","explanation":"Smell.","explanation_ar":"الشم.","options":["The boxes had crescent moons.","The warm samosas smelled of spices.","The call to prayer sounded.","The dates felt sticky."],"dok":1,"skill":"senses","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which simile about the Founding Day fireworks is written correctly?","answer":"The fireworks were as bright as the sun.","explanation":"as … as.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The fireworks were as bright like the sun.","The fireworks were bright as like the sun.","The fireworks were as bright as the sun.","The fireworks were more bright as the sun."],"dok":2,"skill":"similes","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about the street at sunset uses the STRONGEST verb?","answer":"The smell of soup drifted down the street.","explanation":"Strong verb.","explanation_ar":"فعل قوي.","options":["The soup was on the street.","There was soup.","The smell of soup drifted down the street.","The soup went down the street."],"dok":2,"skill":"word choice","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence SHOWS that the workers were thankful?","answer":"The workers smiled, put their hands on their hearts and said, \"Jazakum Allah khairan.\"","explanation":"Show, don't tell.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر الامتنان.","options":["The workers smiled, put their hands on their hearts and said, \"Jazakum Allah khairan.\"","The workers were thankful.","The workers felt thanks.","Thankful workers were there."],"dok":2,"skill":"show, don't tell","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which is the BEST topic sentence for a description of an iftar table?","answer":"At sunset, our iftar table is a rainbow of colors and smells.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"الأفضل.","options":["We eat food.","At sunset, our iftar table is a rainbow of colors and smells.","Iftar is a meal.","My table is wooden."],"dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about the iftar dates uses an exact adjective?","answer":"The dates were soft, sticky and sweet.","explanation":"Exact words.","explanation_ar":"صفات دقيقة.","options":["The dates were nice.","The dates were good.","The dates were OK.","The dates were soft, sticky and sweet."],"dok":3,"skill":"word choice","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a descriptive paragraph (8–10 sentences) about a celebration you love (an Eid gathering, Founding Day, a Ramadan iftar or a family wedding). Use details from all five senses in a clear order, exact adjectives, strong verbs, at least one simile and a closing sentence with a feeling.","model":"At sunset in Ramadan, our iftar table is a rainbow of colors and smells. Bowls of red lentil soup, golden samosas and green salad cover every corner. The smell of cardamom coffee and fresh bread drifts from the kitchen. Everyone sits quietly and waits. Then the call to prayer floats across the roofs like a gentle song. I pick up a soft, sticky date, and it melts in my mouth, as sweet as honey. My little brother gulps his cold water so fast that he coughs, and we all laugh. The clay cup of soup feels warm in my hands. When I look at my family around the table, I feel thankful and full of peace.","marks":20}});
